window.PLAIN = {"scenarios": [{"id": "s1", "title": "Scenario 1 — Customer Support Resolution Agent", "domains": "D1 · D2 · D5", "brief": "A meal-kit company runs a customer support agent on the Claude Agent SDK. Its MCP tools are <code>get_customer</code>, <code>lookup_order</code>, <code>process_refund</code>, <code>escalate_to_human</code> and <code>pause_subscription</code>. The agent may refund up to $150 per box; larger refunds go to a human."}, {"id": "s2", "title": "Scenario 2 — Code Generation with Claude Code", "domains": "D3 · D4 · D5", "brief": "A startup uses Claude Code in one TypeScript repository with <code>server/</code>, <code>mobile/</code> and <code>packages/shared/</code> folders. The repository commits a <code>.claude/</code> directory and a root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. Engineers use Claude Code to generate endpoints, screens and tests from tickets."}, {"id": "s6", "title": "Scenario 6 — Structured Data Extraction", "domains": "D4 · D5 · D1", "brief": "An insurance broker extracts data from certificates of insurance sent by many carriers. Claude returns the insured name, carrier, policy number, limits, deductibles and dates through a tool with a JSON schema. A validator checks each result before it is posted to the broker's system."}, {"id": "s5", "title": "Scenario 5 — Claude Code for Continuous Integration", "domains": "D3 · D4 · D2", "brief": "A travel-booking company runs Claude Code in <b>GitHub Actions</b> on a Kotlin and TypeScript repository. It has four jobs: a pull-request review that posts inline comments, an autofix job for failing builds, test generation for new endpoints, and a weekly dependency-risk report. The review job uses an MCP server for code search and API guidelines. <code>CLAUDE.md</code> and <code>.claude/</code> are committed."}, {"id": "s3", "title": "Scenario 3 — Multi-Agent Research System", "domains": "D1 · D2 · D5", "brief": "A hospital's evidence team runs a research system on the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent hands work to subagents through the Task tool: literature search, trial-registry search, document analysis, citation checking, synthesis and reporting. Journals and the trial registry are reached over MCP. Summaries go to guideline committees, and every claim must cite a paper and section."}, {"id": "s4", "title": "Scenario 4 — Developer Productivity with Claude", "domains": "D2 · D3 · D1", "brief": "A language-learning company's platform team uses Claude Code on a large Ruby on Rails app with a React front end. Engineers read and change code with the built-in tools: Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep and Glob. MCP servers connect Claude to Linear and a staging Postgres database. Some engineers also script Claude with the Agent SDK for internal tools."}], "questions": [{"id": "m7p-s1-03", "was": "m7-s1-03", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Prompt guidance vs programmatic enforcement", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "Customers who want to cancel are handed to a person through <code>escalate_to_human</code>. Product would like the agent to suggest pausing the subscription first, before it hands the customer over. This is a retention idea, not a business rule, so the agent does not have to do it in every chat. What is the <b>most appropriate</b> way to add it?", "options": [{"t": "Add a system-prompt instruction to suggest a pause, plus two short example chats.", "why": "Best. A soft behaviour, where an occasional miss does no harm, is what prompt guidance is for. One instruction and two examples are easy to maintain."}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks <code>escalate_to_human</code> until a pause has been offered.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The hook would make the offer happen every time, but it needs code to spot cancellations and track what was said. Guarantees are for business rules.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add the pause offer to the <code>escalate_to_human</code> description, so Claude offers it before calling the tool.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. Claude does read tool descriptions, but they explain when and how to call a tool. How to talk to the customer belongs in the system prompt, where it is easy to find and change.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Route every cancellation request to a new retention subagent before it reaches a human.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A retention subagent could make the offer well, but it adds a handoff and another prompt to maintain, for one sentence of behaviour.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "This is a retention idea, not a business rule, so the agent does not have to do it in every chat.", "explanation": "Programmatic enforcement is for steps that must happen every time. A best-effort suggestion is shaped with a clear instruction plus a few examples.", "eli5": {"question": "Product wants the agent to ask “would you like to pause instead?” before it hands a cancellation to a person. The clue: it's a retention idea, not a business rule.", "answer": "A shop asks staff to mention the loyalty card at the till. A note and a quick demo do the job; nobody installs a till that locks until the card is mentioned. Here the note is a <b>system-prompt instruction</b> and the demo is <b>example chats</b>. <b>Rule: must happen every time → code. Nice to have → prompt and examples.</b>", "options": ["Correct. A plain instruction plus examples sets the nudge.", "A lock in code (a <b>hook</b>) is for things that must never be skipped. Here skipping is allowed.", "Tool instructions would work, but they're the wrong place for how to talk to customers.", "A whole new helper agent for one friendly sentence is too much."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the most reliable option is always the best one</b>. The question asks for the most <i>appropriate</i> one. Code-level enforcement (a hook) is right when a step must happen every time, such as checking identity before a refund. For a nice-to-have it is extra machinery: something has to detect cancellations and remember what was said.", "diagram": "Must it happen every time?\n  yes → hook or prerequisite gate (code)\n  no  → prompt instruction + examples", "mapTitle": "How firmly must this behaviour hold?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["A suggestion or tone; an occasional miss is harmless", "System-prompt instruction + a few examples", "Cheap and easy to change"], ["A business rule; a miss costs money or breaks policy", "Hook or prerequisite gate in code", "Prompts have a non-zero failure rate"], ["Sensitive data must never reach the model", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook that strips it", "The model can't repeat what it never saw"], ["Guidance on when and how to call a tool", "The tool's description", "Claude reads it when choosing tools"], ["Instructions are clear but output still varies", "Few-shot examples", "Shows the pattern"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Product would like the agent to suggest pausing the subscription first, before it hands the customer over", "means": "The goal is a suggestion in the conversation. That belongs in Claude's instructions, not in a tool's description (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "This is a retention idea, not a business rule, so the agent does not have to do it in every chat", "means": "The decider. Missing it now and then is harmless, so a hook (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a new subagent (<b>[[3]]</b>) is more than the goal needs."}, {"quote": "What is the most appropriate way to add it?", "means": "“Most appropriate” means the right size: an instruction with examples, <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Business rule → enforce it in code. Suggestion or style → prompt instruction plus examples.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Implement multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "Programmatic enforcement (hooks, prerequisite gates) vs prompt-based guidance."}}}, {"id": "m7p-s1-06", "was": "m7-s1-06", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.1 Tool descriptions: input formats", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "Customers usually describe a box by when it arrived, for example “last Tuesday's box”. The agent passes that date to <code>lookup_order</code> as the box ID, and the call fails. Each customer's box IDs are listed in their <code>get_customer</code> record. The description of <code>lookup_order</code> is one line: “Looks up a box.” What is the <b>most effective first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add few-shot examples to the system prompt that turn a delivery date into a box ID first.", "why": "Works, but leaves the cause in place. Examples would teach the date-to-ID step, but they add tokens to every request while the one-line description stays unclear. They are a later step.", "trap": "works around the cause"}, {"t": "Add a routing layer that detects dates and calls <code>get_customer</code> before <code>lookup_order</code>.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt for a first step. Code that spots dates and calls get_customer first would fix this case, but it is fragile and far more work than a better description.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Expand the <code>lookup_order</code> description with the ID format, an example, and where IDs come from.", "why": "Best. Claude learns how to call a tool mainly from its description. This one gives no ID format and no route from a date to an ID, and fixing that is cheap."}, {"t": "Add a <code>lookup_order_by_date</code> tool that takes a delivery date and returns the box.", "why": "Works, but more than a first step. A date-based tool would handle this request, but it is new code, and with equally thin descriptions the agent can misuse it too.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The description of lookup_order is one line: “Looks up a box.”", "explanation": "Tool descriptions are the main signal for how to call a tool. A minimal description should first be expanded with input format, examples, edge cases and boundaries.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent keeps putting a date where a box ID should go. The clue: the tool's instructions are four words long.", "answer": "If a form field just says “Box”, people write all sorts of things in it. Write “Box ID, like BX-20431, found on the customer record” and most mistakes stop. For Claude, the <b>tool description</b> is that label. <b>Rule: tool misused and its description is thin → fix the description first.</b>", "options": ["Examples help later, but the label itself is still unclear.", "A program that hunts for dates in messages is a lot of work for a first step.", "Correct. Fix the label: say what an ID looks like and where to find it.", "A second tool with an equally vague label can go wrong the same way."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the agent needs more examples or smarter routing</b>. But Claude decides how to call a tool mainly from the tool's description. “Looks up a box” gives no ID format and no hint that IDs come from <code>get_customer</code>. Fix that first: it is cheap and it fixes the cause.", "diagram": "\"Looks up a box\" → Claude guesses → date as ID → fail\n+ ID format, example, where IDs come from → right call", "mapTitle": "A tool is called wrongly. What do you fix first?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Where the fault is", "First fix"], "rows": [["Wrong input passed; the description is one line", "Description lacks format and examples", "Expand the description"], ["Two similar tools get mixed up", "Overlapping descriptions", "Sharpen each description's boundaries"], ["Descriptions are detailed; behaviour still varies", "Ambiguous cases", "Add few-shot examples"], ["Agent prefers Grep over a better MCP tool", "The MCP description undersells it", "Improve the MCP tool's description"], ["Bad calls happen and the agent can't recover", "Error says only “failed”", "Structured error naming the expected format"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Customers usually describe a box by when it arrived", "means": "The agent gets a date, so the tool must explain how to get from a date to an ID."}, {"quote": "The agent passes that date to lookup_order as the box ID, and the call fails", "means": "The agent doesn't know what a box ID looks like. That is a description problem, not a routing problem (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Each customer's box IDs are listed in their get_customer record", "means": "The data to fix it already exists. The agent just isn't told where to look, which a description can say."}, {"quote": "The description of lookup_order is one line: “Looks up a box.”", "means": "The decider. The cause is a thin description, so examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a new tool (<b>[[3]]</b>) work around it."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective first step?", "means": "First step = the cheapest change that fixes the cause: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "A tool gets the wrong input and its description is thin? Expand the description first.", "guide": {"obj": "2.1 Design effective tool interfaces with clear descriptions and boundaries", "quote": "Tool descriptions are the primary mechanism LLMs use for selection"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s2-02", "was": "m7-s2-02", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.3 Path-scoped rules", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The team's accessibility conventions apply to every screen file. Screen files are named <code>*.screen.tsx</code> and live in <code>mobile/</code> and <code>packages/shared/ui/</code>. The conventions sit in the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, so they also load during server work. Where should they <b>go</b>?", "options": [{"t": "A <code>CLAUDE.md</code> in <code>mobile/</code>, and one in <code>packages/shared/ui/</code> that imports it.", "why": "Works, but tied to folders. Folder CLAUDE.md files would load the conventions for screens, but they need one per tree and also load for every other file in those folders.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "An <code>/a11y</code> skill that engineers invoke when they work on a screen.", "why": "Works, but only when someone remembers. A skill loads when it is invoked, so screens edited without it miss the conventions.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "A file in <code>.claude/rules/</code> whose <code>paths:</code> frontmatter is <code>**/*.screen.tsx</code>.", "why": "Best. A path-scoped rule loads whenever Claude edits a matching file, in any folder, and stays out of server work."}, {"t": "A separate markdown file pulled into the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> with an <code>@import</code> line.", "why": "Works, but still loads everywhere. @import keeps the conventions in their own file, which is tidier, but they still load in every session, including server work.", "trap": "wrong home"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Screen files … live in mobile/ and packages/shared/ui/.", "explanation": "Conventions tied to a file type that spans folders belong in .claude/rules/ with a paths glob. They load only for matching files, wherever those files are.", "eli5": {"question": "Some rules apply only to screen files, which sit in two different folders. Right now Claude reads those rules even when it works on the server.", "answer": "Picture a sticky note that pops up only when you open a certain kind of file, wherever it is. That's a <b>rule file with a path pattern</b> in <code>.claude/rules/</code>. A folder <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is a note stuck on a folder: fine when everything is in one folder, clumsy when the files are spread out. <b>Rule: convention follows a file pattern → path-scoped rule. Convention follows a folder → folder CLAUDE.md.</b>", "options": ["Notes stuck on two folders: copies to keep in sync, and they show up for other files in those folders too.", "A skill waits until someone asks for it. People forget.", "Correct. The note appears for screen files only, in any folder.", "Importing into the root file still loads it for everyone, every time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>conventions belong in a CLAUDE.md near the code</b>. That works when the code sits in one folder. These conventions follow a <i>file name pattern</i> across two trees. Claude Code can load a rule file only when the file being edited matches a glob, wherever it lives.", "diagram": ".claude/rules/a11y.md   paths: **/*.screen.tsx\n  mobile/home.screen.tsx          → loaded\n  packages/shared/ui/x.screen.tsx → loaded\n  server/api.ts                   → not loaded", "mapTitle": "Where does this instruction live?", "map": {"head": ["Where it applies", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Files matching a pattern, in many folders", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with a <code>paths:</code> glob", "Loads by file, in any folder"], ["Everything inside one folder", "That folder's <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Loads when working there"], ["The whole project, always", "Root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> (or an @import from it)", "Shared with the team through git"], ["Only you, in every project", "<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Personal, not in git"], ["A task someone runs on request", "A skill or slash command", "Loads only when invoked"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team's accessibility conventions apply to every screen file", "means": "The convention follows a kind of file, which is what a <code>paths:</code> glob matches."}, {"quote": "Screen files are named *.screen.tsx and live in mobile/ and packages/shared/ui/", "means": "The decider. Two trees, so folder <code>CLAUDE.md</code> files (<b>[[0]]</b>) need a copy per tree."}, {"quote": "The conventions sit in the root CLAUDE.md, so they also load during server work", "means": "The goal is to stop loading them everywhere. An @import from the root (<b>[[3]]</b>) still loads everywhere."}, {"quote": "Where should they go?", "means": "A rule that loads by itself for matching files: <b>[[2]]</b>. A skill (<b>[[1]]</b>) needs someone to invoke it."}], "rule": "Convention follows a file pattern across folders → .claude/rules/ with a paths glob.", "guide": {"obj": "3.3 Path-specific rules for conditional convention loading", "quote": "Glob-pattern rules beat directory CLAUDE.md for conventions spanning directories"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s6-01", "was": "m7-s6-01", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 Nullable fields against fabrication", "trap": "Prompt where enforcement was required", "fam": 1, "select": 1, "question": "Many certificates do not show a deductible at all. In the schema, deductible is a required number. For these certificates, the extraction returns 0 or copies the coverage limit into the field. Those values are posted as if they were printed, and reviewers cannot tell them apart from real ones. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add two example certificates with no deductible to the prompt, each with its expected output.", "why": "Works, but only partly. Examples show what to do when no deductible is printed, but while the schema demands a number the model has no honest value to return.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Make deductible nullable in the schema, and state in its description when to return null.", "why": "Best. A required number forces the model to invent one. A nullable field gives it an honest way to say “not printed”."}, {"t": "Keep deductible required and have the model enter -1 whenever no deductible is printed.", "why": "Works, but fragile. -1 marks the gap, but it is still a number: every system downstream must know the convention, or -1 posts as a real value.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Add a <code>deductible_printed</code> flag beside the number, and have reviewers ignore the number when it is false.", "why": "Works, but the invented number stays. The flag tells reviewers to ignore it, but the required field still puts a made-up value in the record, where other systems can use it.", "trap": "hides the problem"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "In the schema, deductible is a required number.", "explanation": "Required fields push the model to invent values when the source lacks them. Optional or nullable fields, with a description of when to leave them empty, prevent that.", "eli5": {"question": "When a certificate has no deductible, the extraction makes one up (0, or the limit). The clue: the schema says the field must always hold a number.", "answer": "A form with a box that must contain a number, and no “not applicable” option, makes people write something, anything. Add “leave blank if not printed” and the made-up values stop. In a JSON schema that's a <b>nullable field</b>. <b>Rule: the model invents values when the schema leaves it no honest answer. Make missing values nullable.</b>", "options": ["Showing examples doesn't help while the form still has no blank option.", "Correct. Let the field be empty when nothing is printed.", "-1 is still a number someone can mistake for a real one.", "A “please ignore” sticker helps, but the made-up number is still on the form."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>made-up values are a prompting problem</b>. Here the schema causes them: a required number has no valid way to say “not printed”, so the model fills it. Fix the schema first; examples help only once the schema allows an honest answer.", "diagram": "required number + nothing printed → model invents 0\nnullable number + nothing printed → null (honest)", "mapTitle": "Extraction output is wrong. Schema, prompt or retry?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Values invented when the source lacks them", "The field is required", "Make it optional or nullable"], ["Categories forced into the nearest option", "Closed enum", "Add <code>other</code> plus a detail field"], ["Field allows null but the model still guesses", "Unclear when to leave it empty", "Describe the rule and add examples"], ["Accuracy drops on unusual layouts", "Too few patterns shown", "Few-shot examples of each layout"], ["A value fails a validation check", "The model misread the document", "Retry with the specific error"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Many certificates do not show a deductible at all", "means": "Missing is a normal case, so the output needs a way to say so."}, {"quote": "In the schema, deductible is a required number", "means": "The decider: the schema forces a number. Examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) can't fix a schema with no honest answer, and a flag (<b>[[3]]</b>) leaves the invented number in the record."}, {"quote": "Those values are posted as if they were printed, and reviewers cannot tell them apart from real ones", "means": "Invented values look real. A marker like -1 (<b>[[2]]</b>) has the same problem."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Change the schema: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Model inventing values for missing data? Make the field nullable and say when to use null.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "Optional/nullable fields prevent fabrication"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s1-10", "was": "m7-s1-10", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Case facts across summarisation", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "Some refund disputes go on for a long time. When a chat gets close to the context limit, the SDK replaces older turns with a summary. After that, the agent sometimes offers a smaller refund than it promised earlier. It also forgets which items the customer said were missing. Which change <b>best fixes this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Before each refund offer, have the agent call <code>lookup_order</code> again to reload the order and its items.", "why": "Works, but only for what the order system knows. It reloads the order, but the promised amount and the items the customer reported missing were said in the chat, not stored there.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Raise the threshold for summarising older turns, so more of each chat stays word for word.", "why": "Works, but only delays it. More of each chat stays word for word, but long disputes still cross the threshold and the same facts get blurred.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Tell the summariser to quote every amount and item mentioned in the earlier turns.", "why": "Works, but still lossy. A careful summary keeps most amounts, but the facts go through a summary on every pass, and each pass can drop or change them.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Keep a case-facts block of promised amounts, missing items and order IDs in every request.", "why": "Best. Facts kept outside the summarised history survive every summary, and the agent sees them on every turn."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "After that, the agent sometimes offers a smaller refund than it promised earlier.", "explanation": "Summarising blurs numbers, dates and promises. Pull them into a persistent case-facts block that is sent with every request instead of being summarised.", "eli5": {"question": "To save space, old parts of the chat get squeezed into a summary. After that, the agent forgets what it promised. The clue: the facts get lost <i>in the summary</i>.", "answer": "When a long meeting is summarised, exact numbers get rounded off. A good assistant keeps a separate card with the key facts: “promised $40 credit; salmon and rice missing”. The summary can be rough; the card stays exact. That card is a <b>case-facts block</b> sent with every request. <b>Rule: numbers and promises that must survive go in a facts block, not the summary.</b>", "options": ["Re-reading the order helps, but the promises were made in the chat, not in the order.", "Summarising later only moves the problem further down the chat.", "A better summary is still a summary; numbers can still slip.", "Correct. Keep the key facts on a separate card that never gets summarised."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a better summary</b> will keep the facts. But every summary is a lossy step, and a long chat gets summarised again and again. Exact facts such as amounts, items and promises should not depend on a summary at all. Keep them in a separate block that goes with every request.", "diagram": "older turns   → summary (blurry)     ┐\ncase facts: $40 credit, salmon      ├→ every request\nrecent turns  (word for word)       ┘", "mapTitle": "Facts go missing in a long session. Where are they lost?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Promised amounts or items drift after summarising", "Facts blurred by summaries", "Persistent case-facts block"], ["Context fills fast with long tool output", "Verbose tool results", "Trim results to the needed fields"], ["Details in the middle of a long input are missed", "Lost in the middle", "Key facts first, clear section headers"], ["Claims lose their sources after summarising", "No claim-to-source mapping", "Keep the source with each claim"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "When a chat gets close to the context limit, the SDK replaces older turns with a summary", "means": "Facts from early turns now exist only inside summaries."}, {"quote": "After that, the agent sometimes offers a smaller refund than it promised earlier", "means": "The decider. The loss starts after summarising, so the summary is the cause. Reloading the order (<b>[[0]]</b>) doesn't bring back what was promised in the chat."}, {"quote": "It also forgets which items the customer said were missing", "means": "More than one kind of fact is lost, so a better summary prompt (<b>[[2]]</b>) or a later threshold (<b>[[1]]</b>) only reduces or delays it."}, {"quote": "Which change best fixes this?", "means": "Take the facts out of the summarised history: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Exact facts that must survive a long chat go into a case-facts block, not into summaries.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "Extract transactional facts into a persistent \"case facts\" block"}}}, {"id": "tg-batch-or-now-1", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.5 Blocking work stays synchronous", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The pull-request review job must pass before a branch can merge, and developers wait for its comments. Each review request sends every file in the changed modules, though comments go only on changed lines. A manager wants to move the job to the Message Batches API to halve its cost. Which change <b>best</b> cuts the review's cost?", "options": [{"t": "Move the job to the Message Batches API and poll for its results every minute.", "why": "Works, but blocks the merge. It halves the price, but a batch can take up to 24 hours with no latency promise. Polling faster doesn't make it finish sooner.", "trap": "blocks the merge"}, {"t": "Keep the job on the standard API and send only the diff and changed files.", "why": "Best. Developers are waiting, so the review stays synchronous. Most of the tokens are files the review never comments on, so trimming them cuts the cost."}, {"t": "Send the job as a batch, with a timeout that falls back to a standard call.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Developers still wait out the timeout, slow batches end up paid for twice, and there are now two paths to run and test.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Review only larger pull requests, and let small ones merge without a review.", "why": "Works, but drops coverage. It saves money by not doing the job. Small changes can still break things.", "trap": "drops coverage"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The pull-request review job must pass before a branch can merge, and developers wait for its comments.", "explanation": "Batch processing suits latency-tolerant work, not blocking checks like a pre-merge review. To cut the cost of a blocking job, keep it synchronous and send only what it needs.", "eli5": {"question": "A review that blocks merging costs too much, and a manager wants the cheap batch service. The clue: developers are <i>waiting</i> for it.", "answer": "A half-price dry cleaner that returns shirts “within a day” is no use if you need the shirt for a meeting in an hour. Keep the fast service, and stop sending the whole wardrobe when only one shirt is dirty. <b>Rule: someone waiting → synchronous. To save money, trim what you send.</b>", "options": ["Phoning the slow cleaner every minute doesn't get the shirt back sooner.", "Correct. Fast service, and send only the dirty shirt.", "Trying the slow cleaner first, then paying for express when it's late.", "Saving money by not cleaning some shirts at all."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>batch is simply the cheaper way to call Claude</b>. It is half price because it has no latency promise: results arrive within 24 hours. A check that people wait on can't take that deal. The saving for a blocking job has to come from somewhere else, such as sending fewer tokens.", "diagram": "push -> review (standard API, diff + changed files)\n     -> comments in minutes -> merge", "mapTitle": "Batch or now?", "map": {"head": ["Job", "API", "Why"], "rows": [["Blocks a merge or a person", "Standard (synchronous)", "Batch has no latency promise"], ["Report read days later", "Message Batches API", "Half price, results within 24 hours"], ["Large backfill due next week", "Message Batches API", "Half price, time to spare"], ["Needs tool calls mid-task", "Standard (synchronous)", "A batch request can't loop through tools"], ["Sends far more than it reads", "Trim the request", "Fewer tokens on either API"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The pull-request review job must pass before a branch can merge, and developers wait for its comments", "means": "The decider: a blocking job. Batch (<b>[[0]]</b>) and batch with a fallback (<b>[[2]]</b>) both make people wait."}, {"quote": "Each review request sends every file in the changed modules, though comments go only on changed lines", "means": "Most of the tokens are never used. Trim them: <b>[[1]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Which change best cuts the review's cost?", "means": "Cut cost without dropping reviews, which rules out <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Someone waiting on it? Keep it synchronous, and cut cost by trimming the request.", "guide": {"obj": "4.5 Efficient batch processing strategies", "quote": "Good for non-blocking/latency-tolerant (overnight reports, weekly audits); bad for blocking (pre-merge)."}}}, {"id": "tg-batch-or-now-2", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.5 Batch for latency-tolerant work", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "When a support chat ends, a script sends the transcript to Claude to tag the reason for contact. The quality team reads these tags once a week, at its Friday review. Each tag needs a single call with no tools. The script uses the standard Messages API, one call per chat as it ends. Which change <b>best</b> cuts the tagging cost?", "options": [{"t": "Send each day's transcripts every night as one Message Batches API job.", "why": "Best. Nobody reads the tags until Friday, and each tag is a single call. The batch costs half as much, and results are back within a day."}, {"t": "Tag a random sample of chats each week instead of tagging every chat.", "why": "Works, but drops coverage. It saves money by doing less, and the quality team loses the full picture.", "trap": "drops coverage"}, {"t": "Put twenty transcripts into each request to cut the number of calls made.", "why": "Works, but crowds each request. Fewer calls, but still full price, attention spread across twenty chats, and one odd chat can spoil the rest.", "trap": "crowds each request"}, {"t": "Have the support agent tag the reason itself as the last step of each chat.", "why": "Works, but still full price. The tag moves into the live chat, which still pays the standard rate for speed nobody needs.", "trap": "full price"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "The quality team reads these tags once a week, at its Friday review.", "explanation": "Work nobody waits on, made of single calls with no tools, belongs on the Message Batches API: half the cost, results within 24 hours.", "eli5": {"question": "Every chat gets tagged straight away at full price, but the tags are only read on Friday. The clue: nobody waits for them.", "answer": "If a letter only has to arrive by Friday, you don't send it by express courier. You drop the day's letters in the cheap bulk mail each evening. The <b>Message Batches API</b> is that bulk mail: half the price, delivered within a day. <b>Rule: nobody waiting + one call each → batch.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Bulk mail each evening, still well before Friday.", "Saving stamps by sending only some of the letters.", "Stuffing twenty letters in one express envelope: still express, and they get mixed up.", "Handing letters to the courier at the door: still express price."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>sending each item as soon as it exists is the normal way</b>. It is only needed when someone is waiting. When results are read days later, the instant answer is speed you pay full price for and never use.", "diagram": "chats end -> collect the day's transcripts\n  -> nightly Message Batches job (custom_id per chat)\n  -> tags ready long before Friday", "mapTitle": "Is anyone waiting?", "map": {"head": ["When the result is used", "API", "Cost"], "rows": [["Seconds later, by a person or a merge", "Standard (synchronous)", "Full price"], ["Next day or later", "Message Batches API", "Half price"], ["Next week, in bulk", "Message Batches API", "Half price"], ["Mid-task, after a tool call", "Standard (synchronous)", "Full price"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The quality team reads these tags once a week, at its Friday review", "means": "The decider: nobody waits for a tag. Instant answers (<b>[[3]]</b>) are speed nobody uses."}, {"quote": "Each tag needs a single call with no tools", "means": "A batch request can do this; it doesn't need a tool loop."}, {"quote": "Which change best cuts the tagging cost?", "means": "Cut cost and still tag every chat: <b>[[0]]</b>, not a sample (<b>[[1]]</b>) or crowded requests (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}], "rule": "Nobody waiting, one call each? Send it as a Message Batches API job at half the cost.", "guide": {"obj": "4.5 Efficient batch processing strategies", "quote": "Message Batches API: 50% cost savings, up to 24-hour window, no latency SLA."}}}, {"id": "tg-batch-or-now-3", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.5 Batch has no multi-turn tool calling", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Each quarter the team audits about 3,000 past summaries. For each one, the citation-check subagent fetches the cited sections over MCP, then confirms each claim matches its source. Every summary already lists the papers and sections it cites. The audit is due in two weeks, and the team wants it to cost less. What is the <b>best</b> approach?", "options": [{"t": "Keep running the audit through the subagent on the standard API, as it runs today.", "why": "Works, but full price. It finishes in time, but nobody needs the answers this week, and every call pays the standard rate.", "trap": "full price"}, {"t": "Fetch the quoted passages in code, then batch one request per summary.", "why": "Best. The summaries already say what to fetch, so code can fetch it. Each request then needs no tools, which a batch can run at half the cost."}, {"t": "Batch one request per summary, and send each tool result back in a follow-up batch.", "why": "Works, but slow round trips. A batch request can't loop through tools, so every fetch becomes a new batch that can take up to 24 hours.", "trap": "slow round trips"}, {"t": "Run several citation-check subagents in parallel to finish the audit within a day.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It is fast, but it pays full price and adds coordination for speed nobody needs.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Every summary already lists the papers and sections it cites.", "explanation": "The Message Batches API does not support multi-turn tool calling in a single request. When the data a step needs is known in advance, fetch it in code and send single-call requests as a batch.", "eli5": {"question": "A big, slow-deadline audit uses tool calls to fetch papers. The clue: each summary already says exactly which sections to fetch.", "answer": "The cheap bulk service can't phone you halfway to ask for a missing page. So put every page it needs in the envelope before you post it. Fetching the sections in code first turns each check into one self-contained request, which the <b>Message Batches API</b> runs at half price. <b>Rule: batch requests can't call tools, so hand them everything up front.</b>", "options": ["Keeps working, at express price, for an audit due in two weeks.", "Correct. Put the pages in the envelope, then use bulk mail.", "Posting a letter, waiting a day for a question, posting the answer, and so on.", "Hiring more couriers to finish in a day when you have two weeks."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>an agent job can move to batch as it is</b>. A batch request returns once. If Claude needs a tool result mid-task, the request can't loop. But when the tool calls are predictable, code can make them first, and each request no longer needs a tool.", "diagram": "code: fetch cited sections for each summary\n  -> batch: one request per summary (custom_id)\n  -> results within 24 h, half price", "mapTitle": "Can this job go to batch?", "map": {"head": ["Job shape", "Fit for batch?", "What to do"], "rows": [["One call, no tools, nobody waiting", "Yes", "Batch it"], ["Tool calls Claude must choose as it goes", "No", "Keep it synchronous"], ["Tool calls known in advance", "Yes, after a change", "Fetch in code, then batch"], ["Someone waiting on the answer", "No", "Keep it synchronous"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the citation-check subagent fetches the cited sections over MCP", "means": "Today the job uses tool calls, which a single batch request can't loop through (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Every summary already lists the papers and sections it cites", "means": "The decider: the fetches are known in advance, so code can do them first."}, {"quote": "The audit is due in two weeks, and the team wants it to cost less", "means": "Nobody is waiting. Full price (<b>[[0]]</b>) and parallel speed (<b>[[3]]</b>) buy nothing."}, {"quote": "What is the best approach?", "means": "Fetch in code, then batch: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Batch can't run a tool loop. If the tool calls are predictable, make them in code, then batch.", "guide": {"obj": "4.5 Efficient batch processing strategies", "quote": "Batch API does not support multi-turn tool calling in a single request."}}}, {"id": "tg-context-meter-1", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Trim verbose tool results", "trap": "More room instead of less noise", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "Customers with several boxes often ask about each one in turn. Each <code>lookup_order</code> result holds about forty fields, such as packing station, nutrition data and courier scans. The agent uses only the items, the delivery date, the status and the price. In these chats the context fills early, and older turns are summarised after a few lookups. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Move the agent to a model with a larger context window, so summaries start much later.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. The window still fills with fields nobody reads, just later, and every turn re-sends all of them.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Keep a case-facts block with each box's items, dates and refund amounts outside the summary.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. The facts survive the summaries, but the window still fills with unused fields and the summaries keep coming.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Cut each order lookup down to the few values the agent needs before Claude reads it.", "why": "Best. It removes the cause: about forty fields shrink to the few the agent needs, so the context stops filling early."}, {"t": "Ask the summariser for a longer, more detailed summary that keeps every order field.", "why": "Works, but makes room tighter. A longer summary keeps more, but it takes space the order fields already crowd, so passes still come early.", "trap": "delays the problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The agent uses only the items, the delivery date, the status and the price.", "explanation": "Tool results pile up in the context and use tokens out of all proportion to what the agent needs. Trim them to the relevant fields before they accumulate.", "eli5": {"question": "Every order lookup brings back a huge record, and the chat runs out of room. The clue: the agent only ever uses four of the fields.", "answer": "If a nurse stapled every lab printout into the handover folder, the folder would be too thick to read. Copy the four values that matter instead. <b>Trimming tool results</b> is that copy. <b>Rule: if a tool returns far more than the agent uses, cut it down before it enters the context.</b>", "options": ["A bigger folder holds more printouts. It is still full of pages nobody reads.", "Writing the key facts on a chart keeps them safe, but the folder is still overflowing.", "Correct. Copy the values that matter; leave the printout out.", "A longer retelling takes even more room in a folder that is already full."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the window is too small</b>. Here the window is full of things nobody uses. The guide's line: tool results <b>accumulate and consume tokens disproportionately</b>. Fix what goes in, not how much fits.", "diagram": "lookup_order → ~40 fields → [trim to 4] → context\nbefore: window full after a few lookups\nafter:  window stays low, no summary pass", "mapTitle": "Where is the context going, and what fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you see", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Window fills after a few tool calls", "Verbose tool results", "Trim results to the relevant fields"], ["Amounts and promises drift after a summary", "Facts retold by each summary", "Case-facts block outside the summary"], ["One long input; middle facts missed", "Lost in the middle", "Key facts first + section headers"], ["Claude Code session full of old output", "Old output still in the window", "/compact, plus a scratchpad file"], ["Wish the window were bigger", "Usually one of the above", "Find which one first"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each lookup_order result holds about forty fields", "means": "The weight is in the tool results, not the chat."}, {"quote": "The agent uses only the items, the delivery date, the status and the price", "means": "The decider: most of each result is unused. Cut it: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "older turns are summarised after a few lookups", "means": "A bigger window (<b>[[0]]</b>) or longer summary (<b>[[3]]</b>) leaves the unused fields in place. A facts block (<b>[[1]]</b>) protects facts but not the room."}], "rule": "Window filling with tool output? Trim the results to the fields the agent uses.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "Tool results accumulate and consume tokens disproportionately (40+ fields when 5 are relevant)"}}}, {"id": "tg-context-meter-2", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Facts block across progressive summarisation", "trap": "Better retelling instead of a facts block", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "Long evidence runs pass through many search and analysis steps. To stay inside the context limit, the coordinator replaces older steps with a running summary. Committees now read lines such as “a large trial showed a modest benefit”. The analysis results gave the exact patient count, the effect size and the section. Those details are gone by the time the report is written. Which change <b>best fixes</b> this?", "options": [{"t": "Tell the summariser to copy every number, date and citation exactly as it appears.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A clear instruction cuts the slips, but the summary is still a model rewriting the facts, pass after pass.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Give the coordinator a model with a larger window so it can skip the running summary.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. Longer runs still hit the limit, and until then the findings sit in the middle of a very long context.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Have the report subagent rerun the analysis for each paper it cites in the summary.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It recovers the numbers by doing the analysis twice, at the cost of a second pass over every paper.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Keep each finding's numbers, dates and source in a block passed on unsummarised.", "why": "Best. The exact figures live outside the running summary, so no pass can blur them, and the report reads them as they were."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The analysis results gave the exact patient count, the effect size and the section.", "explanation": "Progressive summarisation condenses numbers, dates and sources into vague text. Keep transactional facts in a persistent structured block that is carried forward unchanged.", "eli5": {"question": "The numbers were right after analysis, then turned into “a large trial” after a few summaries. The clue: the exact details existed and were lost on the way.", "answer": "Retelling a patient's dose each shift turns “40 mg at 6 pm” into “some pain relief”. Writing it on the chart keeps it exact. A <b>facts block</b> passed on unsummarised is the chart. <b>Rule: facts that must stay exact go somewhere no summary rewrites.</b>", "options": ["Asking each nurse to retell carefully helps, but it is still a retelling.", "A longer shift means fewer handovers, not none.", "Re-examining the patient finds the numbers again, but that's a lot of work to redo.", "Correct. Write it on the chart; nobody retells the chart."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a better summary keeps the numbers</b>. Each pass is still a model compressing text, and numbers, dates and sources are the first to go. Put them where no summary touches them.", "diagram": "analysis → finding {n, effect, date, paper §}\n  ├─ running summary: rewritten each pass\n  └─ facts block: copied forward unchanged → report", "mapTitle": "Which part of the context keeps a fact exact?", "map": {"head": ["Where the fact lives", "What happens to it", "Use it for"], "rows": [["Running summary", "Rewritten on every pass", "The story of the run"], ["Facts block outside the summary", "Copied forward word for word", "Numbers, dates, sources, promises"], ["Recent turns", "Exact until the next pass", "Current step only"], ["Middle of a very long context", "Easy to miss", "Nothing you need exact"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the coordinator replaces older steps with a running summary", "means": "Every fact in the history gets retold, again and again."}, {"quote": "The analysis results gave the exact patient count, the effect size and the section", "means": "The decider: the details existed. They are lost in the retelling, so give them a home outside it: <b>[[3]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Those details are gone by the time the report is written", "means": "A firmer instruction (<b>[[0]]</b>) lowers the loss rate; a bigger window (<b>[[1]]</b>) postpones it; redoing the analysis (<b>[[2]]</b>) is costly."}], "rule": "Numbers blurring after summaries? Keep them in a facts block the summary never rewrites.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "Progressive summarization risks condensing numbers, %, dates, customer expectations into vague summaries"}}}, {"id": "tg-context-meter-3", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.4 Scratchpad and /compact in long exploration", "trap": "More room instead of saved findings", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer has spent a long Claude Code session tracing how lessons are priced. Early on, Claude named the exact classes that apply discounts and taxes. Now its answers mention “the usual service objects” instead, and two recent answers contradict each other. The context is nearly full of earlier output. Several pricing questions are still left. What should the engineer <b>do next</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have Claude save its key findings to a notes file, then /compact the session.", "why": "Best. The class names and decisions are kept exact in a file, and /compact clears the old output that is crowding them."}, {"t": "Switch the session to a model with a larger context window and keep asking.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. The early findings stay buried in the middle of a longer session, and the answers keep drifting.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Start a fresh session and ask Claude to explore the pricing code from scratch.", "why": "Works, but throws away work. The early findings were right; a fresh start pays to rediscover them and may drift again later.", "trap": "throws away work"}, {"t": "Add a CLAUDE.md line asking Claude to name the specific classes in each answer.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The instruction asks for names, but the names it found early are what is getting lost.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Now its answers mention “the usual service objects” instead, and two recent answers contradict each other.", "explanation": "Inconsistent answers and talk of typical patterns are signs of context degradation. Persist key findings in a scratchpad file and reduce context use with /compact.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude found the exact classes early, and now speaks vaguely and contradicts itself. The clue: the session is nearly full of old output.", "answer": "When a long meeting runs on, write the decisions on the whiteboard, then clear the table of old papers. A <b>notes file</b> is the whiteboard; <b>/compact</b> clears the table. <b>Rule: save what you found, then make room.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Write the findings down, then clear the clutter.", "A bigger table holds more papers; the decisions are still buried among them.", "Starting the meeting again repeats all the work done so far.", "A rule to quote names helps only if the names are still in front of Claude."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>Claude has forgotten because the window is too small</b>. Vague, contradictory answers are context degradation: early findings are buried under later output. The guide's fix is structure: persist findings in a file, then compact.", "diagram": "early findings → buried under test output\n  → write notes file → /compact → answers cite notes", "mapTitle": "A long Claude Code session is degrading. What fits?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Move", "Why"], "rows": [["Key findings must survive a reset", "Scratchpad file", "Persists across boundaries"], ["Window full of old output", "/compact", "Reduces context usage"], ["Exploration will produce lots of output", "Delegate to a subagent", "Isolates verbose output"], ["Long task may crash midway", "State manifest", "Resume where it stopped"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Early on, Claude named the exact classes that apply discounts and taxes", "means": "The findings exist; they are worth keeping, not rediscovering (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Now its answers mention “the usual service objects” instead, and two recent answers contradict each other", "means": "The decider: classic context degradation. More room (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a rule (<b>[[3]]</b>) does not bring the buried findings back."}, {"quote": "The context is nearly full of earlier output", "means": "Save the findings, then compact: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Answers turning vague in a long session? Save findings to a scratchpad file, then /compact.", "guide": {"obj": "5.4 Manage context in large codebase exploration", "quote": "Scratchpad files persist key findings across boundaries; subagent delegation isolates verbose output"}}}, {"id": "tg-coordinator-split-1", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.2 Narrow task decomposition", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "A committee asks how to prevent falls among hospital inpatients. The coordinator splits the work into three searches: exercise programmes, balance training and strength training. Each subagent returns well-cited findings within its topic. The committee replies that the summary leaves out bed alarms, medication reviews and ward layout. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Give each search subagent wider search terms and a higher limit on papers per topic.", "why": "Works, but it blames the subagents. They did their assigned topics well. Wider terms inside “balance training” still won't turn up bed alarms or ward layout.", "trap": "blames the subagent"}, {"t": "Have synthesis check each draft for missing topics and send out more searches.", "why": "Works, but late. A gap check is a good safety net, yet here it catches the hole only after a full wasted round, and the narrow split repeats on the next request.", "trap": "catches it late"}, {"t": "Have the coordinator split the request by type of prevention first.", "why": "Best. The fault is upstream: all three topics were kinds of exercise. A split by type of prevention (exercise, alarms, medicines, environment) covers the whole question."}, {"t": "Ask the committee to list every subtopic it wants covered in each new request.", "why": "Works, but it moves the coordinator's job onto the committee. Breaking a broad question into parts is exactly what the coordinator is for.", "trap": "shifts the work to the user"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Each subagent returns well-cited findings within its topic.", "explanation": "When every subagent does its assigned job well and whole topics are still missing, the coordinator's decomposition was too narrow. Fix the split, not the subagents.", "eli5": {"question": "Every helper did a good job, and the report still misses whole topics. The clue: all three topics were kinds of exercise.", "answer": "A head chef asked for three dishes: soup, salad and bread. All three were perfect, and there was no main course. You don't retrain the cooks; you fix the chef's list. The <b>coordinator</b> is the chef. <b>Rule: if every subagent succeeded and topics are missing, the split was too narrow.</b>", "options": ["Better searching inside “balance training” still never reaches bed alarms.", "A check at the end finds the hole, but only after a wasted round.", "Correct. Fix the list of dishes, not the cooks.", "Asking the guests to write the menu is the chef's job handed back."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a gap in the report means a subagent did a poor job</b>. Here each subagent did exactly what it was asked. The gap was created one step earlier, when the coordinator chose the topics. Overly narrow decomposition gives incomplete coverage even when every subagent works perfectly.", "diagram": "falls prevention\n  -> exercise | balance | strength   (all one kind)\nmissing: alarms, medicines, ward layout", "mapTitle": "Where did the gap start?", "map": {"head": ["Symptom", "Where it started", "Fix there"], "rows": [["Whole topics missing, each subagent fine", "Coordinator's split", "Split the request more broadly"], ["Subagent ignores a rule the user gave", "Coordinator's Task prompt", "Write the rule into the prompt"], ["Subagent's results are poor for its topic", "The subagent itself", "Improve its prompt or tools"], ["Draft has a thin spot on a covered topic", "One round of research", "Gap check, then re-delegate"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The coordinator splits the work into three searches: exercise programmes, balance training and strength training", "means": "All three topics are kinds of exercise. The split itself leaves out every other kind of prevention."}, {"quote": "Each subagent returns well-cited findings within its topic", "means": "The decider: the subagents worked. Tuning them (<b>[[0]]</b>) fixes something that isn't broken."}, {"quote": "the summary leaves out bed alarms, medication reviews and ward layout", "means": "Whole topics, not thin sections. A gap check (<b>[[1]]</b>) would find them, but only after a wasted round."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Fix the split where it was made: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Every subagent succeeded, yet topics are missing? Fix the coordinator's split.", "guide": {"obj": "1.2 Orchestrate multi-agent systems with coordinator-subagent patterns", "quote": "Risk: overly narrow decomposition → incomplete coverage of broad topics."}}}, {"id": "tg-coordinator-split-2", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Complete prior findings in the Task prompt", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "When the searches finish, the coordinator writes a short paragraph about the papers they found and sends it to the analysis subagent. Paper titles appear inside sentences, and section numbers are left out. The analysis subagent's findings often cite the wrong paper or no section at all. The citation checker then rejects them. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Let the analysis subagent run its own journal searches and find each of the papers again.", "why": "Works, but it repeats finished work. The searches already found the papers; the coordinator just didn't pass them on in a usable form.", "trap": "duplicate work"}, {"t": "Send the findings to it as a list, with paper titles and section numbers as fields.", "why": "Best. The subagent inherits nothing, so the coordinator passes the complete findings, with the citation details kept apart from the text where they can't be lost."}, {"t": "Add a rule to the analysis subagent's prompt to cite a paper and section for each claim.", "why": "Works, but it blames the subagent. It can't cite a section number it was never sent. The gap is in the prompt the coordinator wrote.", "trap": "blames the subagent"}, {"t": "Have the citation checker find the right paper and section for each claim it rejects.", "why": "Works, but at the end of the line. It repairs citations after the fact, one by one, instead of sending the right details in the first place.", "trap": "catches it late"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Paper titles appear inside sentences, and section numbers are left out.", "explanation": "Subagents receive only what the coordinator writes into the Task prompt. Pass prior agents' complete findings, in a structured form that keeps metadata such as DOI and section apart from the content.", "eli5": {"question": "The analyst cites the wrong papers. The clue: the coordinator's note mixed the titles into sentences and dropped the section numbers.", "answer": "If a manager hands a new colleague a chatty note instead of the file, the colleague can't quote page numbers that weren't in the note. Hand over the file, with the labels on. A subagent starts empty: it knows only the <b>Task prompt</b>. <b>Rule: pass complete findings, with the citation details in their own fields.</b>", "options": ["Sending the colleague to fetch every file again wastes the work already done.", "Correct. Hand over the file with its labels.", "Telling the colleague to quote pages doesn't help if the pages were never handed over.", "Fixing the quotes afterwards is slower than sending them right the first time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a subagent that cites badly needs a stricter instruction</b>. But a subagent knows only what is in its Task prompt. Here the coordinator summarised the search results into prose and dropped the section numbers, so the details the citations need never arrived.", "diagram": "search results -> coordinator prose (no sections)\n               -> analysis subagent -> bad citations", "mapTitle": "What the coordinator should hand on", "map": {"head": ["Need downstream", "Put in the Task prompt", "Why"], "rows": [["Cite paper and section", "Each finding with title, DOI, section fields", "Metadata stays attached to the content"], ["Apply this request's rules", "The rules agreed with the user", "Subagents don't see the coordinator's chat"], ["Build on earlier work", "Complete prior-agent findings", "No re-searching, nothing lost in a summary"], ["Do the job well", "The goal and quality criteria", "Lets the subagent choose how"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the coordinator writes a short paragraph about the papers they found", "means": "Prose written by the coordinator, not the complete findings."}, {"quote": "Paper titles appear inside sentences, and section numbers are left out", "means": "The decider: the citation details were mixed in or dropped before the subagent saw them. A citing rule (<b>[[2]]</b>) can't bring them back."}, {"quote": "every claim must cite a paper and section", "means": "The details that went missing are exactly the ones every claim needs."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Send the findings complete, with metadata in separate fields: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Subagents inherit nothing. Pass complete findings, with citation details in their own fields.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "Include complete prior-agent findings in the subagent prompt; use structured formats to separate content from metadata (URLs, doc names, page numbers) for attribution"}}}, {"id": "tg-coordinator-split-3", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Goals and quality criteria, not step lists", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The coordinator writes each search Task prompt as fixed steps: search the drug name with the outcome, take the first ten results, and summarise each one. Subagents follow the steps exactly. They miss trials that use a brand name or another word for the outcome. They also summarise reviews the committee did not need. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "State the goal and what counts as good evidence, and let each subagent plan its searches.", "why": "Best. With the goal and a quality bar, a subagent can adapt: try brand names, skip reviews nobody asked for, and stop when the evidence is good enough."}, {"t": "Add steps to also search brand names and outcome synonyms, and take the first twenty results.", "why": "Works, but it patches the list. This request's misses go away; the next request will find a gap the steps didn't foresee.", "trap": "patches the step list"}, {"t": "Give the search subagents a synonym tool that expands each drug name before they search.", "why": "Works, but it blames the subagents' tools. Brand names get found; the subagents still take the first ten results and still don't know which papers the committee needs.", "trap": "blames the subagent"}, {"t": "Have the analysis subagent drop summarised papers that don't answer the committee's question.", "why": "Works, but late, and only half. It removes unneeded reviews after they were summarised, and it can't add the trials the steps missed.", "trap": "catches it late"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Subagents follow the steps exactly.", "explanation": "Coordinator prompts should state goals and quality criteria, not step-by-step procedures. Subagents that know what good looks like can adapt their searches; subagents given a recipe follow it, gaps and all.", "eli5": {"question": "The helpers did exactly what the steps said, and missed what mattered. The clue: they follow the steps exactly.", "answer": "Tell a helper “buy the first ten items on aisle 3” and they'll come back with ten items. Tell them “we're cooking for six, one is vegetarian, keep it under $40” and they'll shop well. The <b>goal and quality bar</b> let them adapt. <b>Rule: brief subagents with goals and quality criteria, not recipes.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Say what you need and what good looks like.", "A longer recipe fixes today's miss; tomorrow brings a new one.", "A better tool helps, but the helper still follows the recipe.", "Throwing out the wrong shopping later doesn't bring back what was never bought."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a subagent that misses things needs more instructions or a better tool</b>. Here the subagents followed their steps exactly; the steps were the problem. A step list can't foresee every request. A goal and a quality bar let the subagent decide how to search.", "diagram": "steps:  search X -> first 10 -> summarise  (rigid)\ngoal:   what the committee needs + quality bar\n        -> subagent plans its own searches", "mapTitle": "Recipe or goal?", "map": {"head": ["Brief style", "What the subagent does", "Result"], "rows": [["Fixed steps", "Follows them exactly", "Misses whatever the steps didn't foresee"], ["Longer fixed steps", "Follows more of them", "Fixes today's miss only"], ["Goal + quality criteria", "Plans and adapts its searches", "Finds what the goal needs"], ["Goal only, no criteria", "Adapts, but guesses the bar", "Uneven depth and sources"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The coordinator writes each search Task prompt as fixed steps", "means": "The brief is a recipe. The coordinator decides how, so the subagent can't adapt."}, {"quote": "Subagents follow the steps exactly", "means": "The decider: the subagents did what they were told. A new tool (<b>[[2]]</b>) works on the wrong layer."}, {"quote": "They miss trials that use a brand name or another word for the outcome", "means": "More steps (<b>[[1]]</b>) cover this case; the next request brings another one."}, {"quote": "They also summarise reviews the committee did not need", "means": "They don't know what the committee needs. Filtering later (<b>[[3]]</b>) can't add what was missed."}], "rule": "Brief subagents with the goal and quality criteria, not a step list.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "write coordinator prompts as goals + quality criteria, not step-by-step procedures."}}}, {"id": "tg-extraction-honesty-1", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 tool_use with a JSON schema", "trap": "Fixing text instead of using a tool", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "An internal Agent SDK script reads each incident write-up in Linear. It asks Claude to reply with a JSON object holding the service, severity, start time and root cause. A parser then reads the reply. On some write-ups Claude adds a sentence before the JSON or drops a closing bracket. The parser skips those incidents. The values themselves are usually right. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a complete example reply to the prompt that shows the JSON and nothing else.", "why": "Works, but only usually. An example cuts the stray sentences, but text replies can still break, and each break loses an incident.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Resend any reply that fails to parse, along with the error from the parser.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. The retry usually fixes the reply, but every broken reply costs a second call that a schema would avoid.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Define a <code>record_incident</code> tool with a JSON schema and have Claude call it.", "why": "Best. Tool input arrives as structured JSON that matches the schema, so syntax errors and stray sentences go away at the source."}, {"t": "Make the parser cut out the text between the first and last brace before reading.", "why": "Works, but only for one kind of break. It drops the stray sentence, but a missing bracket still fails, and the next new quirk will too.", "trap": "catches only some cases"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "On some write-ups Claude adds a sentence before the JSON or drops a closing bracket.", "explanation": "Tool use with a JSON schema is the most reliable way to get schema-compliant output; it removes JSON syntax errors. Prompts, retries and lenient parsers only reduce them.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude writes the JSON as text, and sometimes the text breaks. The clue: the values are right; only the format fails.", "answer": "Asking someone to write a form out by hand, neatly, mostly works. Handing them a form with boxes always gives you the boxes. A <b>tool with a JSON schema</b> is the form with boxes. <b>Rule: if structured output breaks as text, return it through a tool.</b>", "options": ["A neat example helps, but handwriting still slips sometimes.", "Sending messy forms back works, but costs a second round each time.", "Correct. Use the form with boxes.", "Tidying up the handwriting fixes one kind of mess, not every kind."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>better instructions will fix the format</b>. Text replies are probabilistic; a tool call with a JSON schema is structured by design. The guide: tool use with a schema <b>eliminates JSON syntax errors</b>. It does not fix wrong values, but here the values are fine.", "diagram": "text reply:  “Here it is: {…”  → parser fails\ntool call:   record_incident({service, …})\n             → structured input, no parsing", "mapTitle": "What goes wrong, and which layer fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["Problem", "Layer", "Fix"], "rows": [["JSON breaks: stray text, missing bracket", "Output format", "Tool with a JSON schema"], ["Field has no value on the page", "Schema design", "Optional or nullable field"], ["Category not in the list", "Schema design", "Enum with other + detail"], ["Value on the page, extracted wrong", "Validation loop", "Retry with the specific error"], ["Odd layouts read wrong", "Prompt", "Worked examples of those layouts"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It asks Claude to reply with a JSON object", "means": "The output is text, so its syntax can break."}, {"quote": "On some write-ups Claude adds a sentence before the JSON or drops a closing bracket", "means": "The decider: syntax failures. A tool call removes them: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "The values themselves are usually right", "means": "Nothing to fix in the content. An example (<b>[[0]]</b>), a retry (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a lenient parser (<b>[[3]]</b>) works around the format instead of removing the cause."}], "rule": "Structured output breaking as text? Return it through a tool with a JSON schema.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "tool_use with JSON schemas = most reliable schema-compliant output (eliminates JSON syntax errors)"}}}, {"id": "tg-extraction-honesty-2", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 Nullable fields against fabrication", "trap": "Forcing a value that isn't there", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The document-analysis subagent extracts each paper's details through a tool with a JSON schema. The schema makes <code>trial_registration_id</code> a required string. Many older papers do not print a registration ID. For those papers, the subagent returns IDs in the right format that do not exist. Reviewers then spend time searching the registry for them. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a prompt rule telling the subagent not to guess an ID that isn't printed.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The rule lowers the guessing, but the schema still demands a string, so the model still has to put something there.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Make <code>trial_registration_id</code> nullable, to be left null when not printed.", "why": "Best. The schema now has an honest answer for “not printed”, so the model has no reason to invent an ID."}, {"t": "Have the citation-check subagent look up every extracted ID in the registry.", "why": "Works, but catches it late. The lookup finds the fake IDs, but they are still invented first, and every paper costs an extra registry call.", "trap": "catches it late"}, {"t": "Add worked examples of papers both with and without a printed registration ID.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Examples show the right behaviour, but a required field still leaves no valid way to say “none”.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The schema makes trial_registration_id a required string.", "explanation": "When a field may be absent from the source, make it optional or nullable. A required field pushes the model to fabricate a plausible value.", "eli5": {"question": "Old papers have no registration ID, but the form insists on one, so the model makes one up. The clue: the field is required.", "answer": "A screen that won't save until every box is filled gets fake answers in the boxes. Add a “not given” option and people stop faking. A <b>nullable field</b> is that option. <b>Rule: if a value may be missing, let the schema say so.</b>", "options": ["Asking people not to fake helps, but the box still has to be filled.", "Correct. Give the box a “not given” option.", "Checking every answer later finds the fakes after they were made.", "Showing examples helps, but the screen still won't save an empty box."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the model needs to be told not to invent</b>. Here the schema is what demands a value. Give the schema an honest answer for “not printed” and the pressure to invent goes away.", "diagram": "required string, nothing printed → plausible fake ID\nnullable, nothing printed        → null (honest)", "mapTitle": "A value may be missing. How does the schema handle it?", "map": {"head": ["Schema choice", "What the model returns", "Effect"], "rows": [["Required field", "A plausible value", "Fabrication that looks real"], ["Sentinel such as 0 or N/A", "A fake value", "Read downstream as real"], ["Optional or nullable field", "null", "Honest gap, easy to route"], ["Enum with other + detail", "other, plus what it is", "Honest new category"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The schema makes trial_registration_id a required string", "means": "The decider: the schema has no valid way to say “not printed”. Make it nullable: <b>[[1]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Many older papers do not print a registration ID", "means": "The value is absent from the source, so a required field forces a guess."}, {"quote": "the subagent returns IDs in the right format that do not exist", "means": "Fabrication. A rule (<b>[[0]]</b>) or examples (<b>[[3]]</b>) fight the schema; a registry check (<b>[[2]]</b>) catches fakes after they're made."}], "rule": "A value may be missing? Make the field optional or nullable, not required.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "Optional/nullable fields prevent fabrication; enum \"unclear\"/\"other\" + detail for extensible categories"}}}, {"id": "tg-extraction-honesty-3", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.4 Retries can't recover absent information", "trap": "Retrying the impossible", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The broker's system needs a policy number on every certificate. When none is extracted, the pipeline retries with the specific error, up to two times. On some scanned certificates, the policy number is cut off at the page edge. These fail each retry. Sometimes the last retry returns a number that is not on the page, and it gets posted. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Raise the retry limit and say in the message that the number must be found.", "why": "Works, but presses harder for the impossible. More retries help only when the number is on the page; here they invite more invented numbers.", "trap": "retrying the impossible"}, {"t": "Check each policy number against the carrier's format before it is posted.", "why": "Works, but catches only some cases. A broken number fails the check, but an invented one in the right format passes.", "trap": "catches only some cases"}, {"t": "Add worked examples that show where each carrier prints its policy number.", "why": "Works, but a different problem. Examples help find a number that is printed somewhere unusual; this one is not on the page.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Return null when the policy number isn't printed and send it to a person.", "why": "Best. A retry can't recover what the scan lost. Null says so honestly, and a person can get the number from the carrier."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "On some scanned certificates, the policy number is cut off at the page edge.", "explanation": "Retries with error feedback fix values that are in the document. When the information is absent from the source, retries fail or invite fabrication; return null and route to human review.", "eli5": {"question": "The policy number is torn off the scan, and the system keeps asking for it until the model makes one up. The clue: the number isn't on the page.", "answer": "If the date is torn off a form, telling the temp “find it” three times won't bring it back. It might get you a made-up date. Hand that form to someone who can phone the customer. <b>Rule: retry only when the answer is on the page. If it isn't, say so and send it to a person.</b>", "options": ["Asking more firmly makes a made-up answer more likely, not a real one.", "Checking the format catches messy numbers, not neat fakes.", "Examples help find a number printed in an odd place, not one that's missing.", "Correct. Say it's missing and hand it to someone who can find it."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a specific retry fixes any validation failure</b>. It fixes values that are on the page but extracted wrong. When the information is absent, every retry fails, and pressure to fill the field produces an invention.", "diagram": "scan: Policy NG-44▒▒▒▒\n  retry “policy number required” → NG-448126 (fake)\n  null + person                  → real number found", "mapTitle": "Validation failed. Is the value on the page?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["On the page, wrong field or format", "Retry with the specific error", "The model can correct it"], ["On the page, odd layouts every time", "Worked examples", "Fixes the first pass"], ["Not on the page", "Null, then a person", "No retry can find it"], ["Invented value in the right format", "Not caught by format checks", "Prevent it at the schema"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the pipeline retries with the specific error", "means": "The retry is already the good kind. Its content is not the problem."}, {"quote": "On some scanned certificates, the policy number is cut off at the page edge", "means": "The decider: the information is absent from the source. Examples (<b>[[2]]</b>) and more retries (<b>[[0]]</b>) can't bring it back."}, {"quote": "the last retry returns a number that is not on the page, and it gets posted", "means": "Fabrication under pressure. A format check (<b>[[1]]</b>) passes a well-formed fake. Return null and route it: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Value missing from the source? Don't retry; return null and send it to a person.", "guide": {"obj": "4.4 Validation, retry, and feedback loops for extraction quality", "quote": "Retries are ineffective when info is absent from the source (vs format/structural errors)"}}}, {"id": "tg-instruction-home-1", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.1 User-level memory for personal preferences", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "A developer added a line to the committed root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. It says: “Explain every change in detail, I am new to Kotlin.” Now the CI review job posts long teaching comments on every pull request. Teammates' own sessions have started explaining everything too. The developer still wants detailed answers in all of their own sessions. Where should the line <b>live</b>?", "options": [{"t": "In a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file whose <code>paths:</code> glob matches only the Kotlin files.", "why": "Works, but still shared. The rule is committed, so CI and teammates load it whenever a Kotlin file is touched, which is most of the time.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "In the developer's own <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>, removed from the committed project file.", "why": "Best. User-level memory is personal and not shared through git. It loads in every one of the developer's sessions, in every project, and nobody else's."}, {"t": "In a project skill that the developer invokes at the start of each of their sessions.", "why": "Works, but only when remembered. It is also committed, so the skill shows up for the whole team.", "trap": "only when invoked"}, {"t": "In the CI job's <code>-p</code> prompt, as an override asking for short review comments.", "why": "Works, but for CI only. The review gets shorter, yet teammates' sessions still load the line from the root file.", "trap": "treats one symptom"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The developer still wants detailed answers in all of their own sessions.", "explanation": "A personal preference belongs in user-level ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md. It is not shared through version control, so it changes only that person's sessions.", "eli5": {"question": "One developer's personal wish (“explain everything to me”) went into the shared file. Now CI and teammates get it too.", "answer": "A sticky note on the office noticeboard is read by everyone. A note in your own notebook is read only by you, wherever you go. The root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is the noticeboard; <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> is your notebook. <b>Rule: only for me → user-level memory. For the team → the project file.</b>", "options": ["A note that pops up on Kotlin files is still on the noticeboard: CI and teammates see it.", "Correct. Your own notebook: you get it everywhere, and nobody else does.", "A note you must remember to fetch each morning, and the whole team can see it.", "Telling CI to ignore it helps CI, but teammates still read the noticeboard."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the problem is CI's output</b>, so the fix goes in CI. The real fault is the <i>home</i> of the line: a personal wish sits in a file that every clone and every CI run loads. Move it to the file that only this person loads.", "diagram": "~/.claude/CLAUDE.md  → this developer, every project\nrepo/CLAUDE.md       → every teammate and every CI run", "mapTitle": "Who should this instruction reach?", "map": {"head": ["Who it is for", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Only me, in every project", "<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Personal, not in git"], ["The whole team and CI, always", "Root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Committed, loads for everyone"], ["Files matching a pattern", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "Committed, loads for matching files"], ["A task run on request", "A skill or slash command", "Loads only when invoked"], ["One CI job's run", "That job's <code>-p</code> prompt", "Affects that run only"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "A developer added a line to the committed root CLAUDE.md", "means": "Committed means shared: every clone and CI load it."}, {"quote": "Teammates' own sessions have started explaining everything too", "means": "The leak is not only in CI, so a CI-only override (<b>[[3]]</b>) fixes one symptom."}, {"quote": "The developer still wants detailed answers in all of their own sessions", "means": "The decider: personal, and in every project. A committed rule (<b>[[0]]</b>) or skill (<b>[[2]]</b>) is still shared, and stays in this repo."}, {"quote": "Where should the line live?", "means": "In the file only this developer loads: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Only for me, everywhere → ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md. For the team and CI → the committed project CLAUDE.md.", "guide": {"obj": "3.1 CLAUDE.md hierarchy, scoping, modular organization", "quote": "User-level is not shared via version control."}}}, {"id": "tg-instruction-home-2", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.2 Project commands shared through git", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer wrote a <code>/new-screen</code> command that creates a screen, its test and its story in the team's layout. It lives in their <code>~/.claude/commands/</code>. Teammates copied it from a chat message, and the copies have drifted, so new screens come out in different layouts. The team wants one version that every engineer gets with the code. Where should the command <b>live</b>?", "options": [{"t": "In the repository's <code>.claude/commands/</code> folder, committed alongside the code.", "why": "Best. Project commands live in the repository, so every engineer gets the same version on clone or pull, and changes go through review."}, {"t": "In the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, as a section that lists the steps for a new screen.", "why": "Works, but wrong kind of file. Claude can follow the steps, but they load into every session and there is no <code>/new-screen</code> to run.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "In a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file whose <code>paths:</code> glob matches <code>*.screen.tsx</code>.", "why": "Works, but loads too late. A path rule loads when a matching file is touched, and the screen does not exist until the command creates it.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "In a team dotfiles repo that copies it into each engineer's <code>~/.claude/commands/</code>.", "why": "Works, but outside the project. Each engineer must install and update the dotfiles, so versions can drift again.", "trap": "copies to maintain"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "The team wants one version that every engineer gets with the code.", "explanation": "A command the whole team should share lives in the project's .claude/commands/, committed to git. ~/.claude/commands/ is for personal commands.", "eli5": {"question": "A handy command lives on one person's laptop. Everyone copied it, and the copies no longer match.", "answer": "If the whole crew needs the same tool, leave it in the shared toolshed on site, not in one person's van. The repository's <code>.claude/commands/</code> is the shared toolshed: whoever clones gets the same tool, and any change is reviewed. <b>Rule: a team command → <code>.claude/commands/</code> in the repo. A personal command → <code>~/.claude/commands/</code>.</b>", "options": ["Correct. One tool in the shared toolshed; everyone gets the same one.", "Written instructions on the wall aren't a tool you can pick up, and everyone reads them all day.", "A note that appears only next to finished screens can't help you make a new one.", "Copying the tool into every van works until someone forgets to update theirs."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the drift is a sharing problem to solve with a sync tool</b>. Claude Code already has a shared home for commands: the repository's <code>.claude/commands/</code>. Committed there, the command arrives with the code and changes go through review.", "diagram": "repo/.claude/commands/new-screen.md → every engineer\n~/.claude/commands/new-screen.md     → one engineer", "mapTitle": "Who needs the command, and where does it live?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Whole team, this repository", "<code>.claude/commands/</code>, committed", "Same version for everyone, reviewed"], ["Only me, every repository", "<code>~/.claude/commands/</code>", "Personal, follows me"], ["A standard that always applies", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Always loaded, not invoked"], ["Conventions for a kind of file", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "Loads when a matching file is touched"], ["Shared copies kept in sync by a script", "Dotfiles repo", "Outside the project; can drift"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It lives in their ~/.claude/commands/", "means": "A user-level command: personal, so teammates had to copy it."}, {"quote": "the copies have drifted, so new screens come out in different layouts", "means": "Any copy-based fix, such as a dotfiles sync (<b>[[3]]</b>), keeps the copies."}, {"quote": "The team wants one version that every engineer gets with the code", "means": "The decider: shared through the repository. A CLAUDE.md section (<b>[[1]]</b>) is shared but isn't a command."}, {"quote": "Where should the command live?", "means": "It must run on request before any screen exists, which rules out a path rule (<b>[[2]]</b>). Answer: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Team command → .claude/commands/ in the repo. Personal command → ~/.claude/commands/.", "guide": {"obj": "3.2 Custom slash commands and skills", "quote": "Project commands in .claude/commands/ (shared) vs user ~/.claude/commands/ (personal)"}}}, {"id": "tg-instruction-home-3", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.1 @import for modular references", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The team's API style guide lives in <code>docs/api-style.md</code>, and the API team updates it often. The root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> holds a pasted copy of the guide. That copy is months out of date, so Claude follows rules the guide has since changed. The guide applies to every endpoint in the app. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Replace the copy with an <code>@import</code> line naming the guide file in <code>docs/</code>.", "why": "Best. The import pulls the live guide into memory, so there is one copy to maintain and Claude always reads the current rules."}, {"t": "Add a CI step that copies the latest guide into <code>CLAUDE.md</code> after every merge.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It keeps the copy fresh with a moving part you must build and maintain, when an import line does it with no copy.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Move the guide into an <code>/api-style</code> skill that engineers invoke for endpoint work.", "why": "Works, but only when invoked. Endpoints written without the skill miss the guide, and the skill is another copy.", "trap": "only when invoked"}, {"t": "Paste the guide into a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file with a <code>paths:</code> glob on the controllers.", "why": "Works, but still a copy. It loads more precisely, yet it drifts from <code>docs/</code> exactly like the pasted copy did.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "That copy is months out of date, so Claude follows rules the guide has since changed.", "explanation": "@import lets CLAUDE.md reference a file instead of copying it, so memory stays in step with the source document.", "eli5": {"question": "The rule book was photocopied into Claude's notes. The real rule book changed; the photocopy didn't.", "answer": "Instead of a photocopy pinned to the wall, pin a note that says “read the rule book on shelf 3”. Whoever reads the note always gets the current book. An <code>@import</code> line in <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is that note. <b>Rule: the content already lives in a file → import it, don't copy it.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Point at the real book, so there's nothing to keep in sync.", "A machine that re-photocopies the book after every change works, but it's a machine to keep running.", "Locking the book in a cupboard means people only read it when they remember to open the cupboard.", "A neater photocopy in a better spot is still a photocopy that goes stale."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>this is about where the guide should load</b>. The guide applies to every endpoint, so always-loaded memory is already the right place. The fault is that memory holds a <i>copy</i>. <code>@import</code> makes <code>CLAUDE.md</code> point at the source file instead.", "diagram": "CLAUDE.md:  @docs/api-style.md\n            ↓ loads the current file\ndocs/api-style.md  (the API team edits only this)", "mapTitle": "Copy it, import it, or load it somewhere else?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Content already lives in a repo file", "<code>@import</code> it from <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "One source, no copy to drift"], ["Conventions for a file pattern across folders", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "Loads only for matching files"], ["A workflow run now and then", "A skill", "Loads only when invoked"], ["Only you need it", "<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Personal, not shared"], ["Memory doesn't seem to load", "Check with <code>/memory</code>", "Shows which files loaded"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team's API style guide lives in docs/api-style.md, and the API team updates it often", "means": "The source of truth is a file that changes."}, {"quote": "The root CLAUDE.md holds a pasted copy of the guide", "means": "Memory holds a copy, not the source."}, {"quote": "That copy is months out of date", "means": "The decider: drift. Another copy (<b>[[2]]</b>, <b>[[3]]</b>) drifts the same way; a sync job (<b>[[1]]</b>) is extra machinery."}, {"quote": "The guide applies to every endpoint in the app", "means": "Always-loaded memory is the right place; only the copy is wrong. Import the file: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Content already in a repo file? @import it from CLAUDE.md instead of pasting a copy.", "guide": {"obj": "3.1 CLAUDE.md hierarchy, scoping, modular organization", "quote": "@import syntax for modular references"}}}, {"id": "tg-mcp-where-what-1", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 Project .mcp.json with env var expansion", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "One engineer connected Claude to the Linear MCP server through their own <code>~/.claude.json</code>, with their API key written in it. Teammates now want the same Linear tools, and so does a new CI job. Each person and the CI job have their own key. No key may be committed to the repository. How should the team <b>set it up</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have each engineer add the server to their own <code>~/.claude.json</code>, as a README describes.", "why": "Works, but personal. Each engineer configures it by hand, the entries drift, and the CI job needs its own setup too.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Add the server to a committed <code>.mcp.json</code> that reads the key from <code>${LINEAR_API_KEY}</code>.", "why": "Best. Project <code>.mcp.json</code> is shared through git, so every clone and CI get the server, and each sets the variable from its own environment or secret store."}, {"t": "Add a script to the repo that calls the Linear API, and let Claude run it through Bash.", "why": "Works, but loses the MCP tools. Claude gets one script instead of described tools with structured errors, and the team maintains it.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Copy the engineer's server entry into the CI runner's <code>~/.claude.json</code> as well.", "why": "Works, but for CI only. Teammates still have nothing, and the server now has two hand-made entries to keep in step.", "trap": "covers only CI"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "No key may be committed to the repository.", "explanation": "Shared MCP servers belong in the project-level .mcp.json. Env var expansion such as ${LINEAR_API_KEY} keeps secrets out of the committed file.", "eli5": {"question": "One engineer's laptop knows how to reach Linear. The team and CI want it too, but nobody's key may go into the repo.", "answer": "Put the address book in the shared office, but leave a blank where the door code goes. Each person fills in their own code from their own pocket. The committed <code>.mcp.json</code> is the shared address book; <code>${LINEAR_API_KEY}</code> is the blank. <b>Rule: a server the team shares → project <code>.mcp.json</code>, with secrets as environment variables.</b>", "options": ["Everyone writing their own address book works, but they drift, and CI needs one too.", "Correct. One shared address book, with each person's code filled in from their own pocket.", "A hand-written phone script works, but it is clumsier than the real phone and someone has to maintain it.", "Giving a copy to CI helps CI only; the teammates still have nothing."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a secret means the config must stay personal</b>. Claude Code separates the two: the server entry goes in the committed project <code>.mcp.json</code>, and the secret stays outside git as an environment variable that the file expands.", "diagram": ".mcp.json (in git):  \"LINEAR_API_KEY\": \"${LINEAR_API_KEY}\"\nlaptop env / CI secret → fills in the real key", "mapTitle": "Who needs the server, and where is it configured?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Whole team and CI, needs a secret", "Project <code>.mcp.json</code> with <code>${VAR}</code>", "Shared config, no committed secret"], ["Whole team, no secret needed", "Project <code>.mcp.json</code>", "Arrives with the clone"], ["Only me, or an experiment", "User-level <code>~/.claude.json</code>", "Personal, not shared"], ["A standard service like Jira or Linear", "An existing community server", "No custom code to maintain"], ["Only one CI job needs it", "Still <code>.mcp.json</code>; CI sets the variable", "One config for all"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "One engineer connected Claude to the Linear MCP server through their own ~/.claude.json", "means": "User-level config: personal, so nobody else has it."}, {"quote": "Teammates now want the same Linear tools, and so does a new CI job", "means": "Shared need. Per-person entries (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a CI-only copy (<b>[[3]]</b>) each cover part of it."}, {"quote": "No key may be committed to the repository", "means": "The decider: the committed file names a variable, not the key: <b>[[1]]</b>."}, {"quote": "How should the team set it up?", "means": "Keep the MCP server and its tools; a Bash script (<b>[[2]]</b>) replaces them with something weaker."}], "rule": "Team-shared MCP server → project .mcp.json, secrets as ${VARS}. Personal or experimental → ~/.claude.json.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "Env var expansion in .mcp.json (e.g., ${GITHUB_TOKEN}) — no committed secrets."}}}, {"id": "tg-mcp-where-what-2", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 Community MCP servers for standard integrations", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "The review job should read the Jira ticket linked in each pull request and check the change against it. It also needs to search related tickets and add a comment. These are ordinary Jira tasks. A developer plans to write the team's own MCP server for them. What is the <b>best approach</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Write the planned server, with detailed tool descriptions and structured errors.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A well-made custom server does the job, but the team now builds and maintains a Jira client that already exists.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Give the job a script that calls the Jira REST API with <code>curl</code> through Bash.", "why": "Works, but fragile. Claude gets raw HTTP output instead of described tools, and the script grows with every new need.", "trap": "fragile"}, {"t": "Configure an existing community Jira MCP server in the project <code>.mcp.json</code>.", "why": "Best. Jira is a standard integration with maintained community servers. Configured in the project file, the review job and every developer get it."}, {"t": "Add the Jira calls as new tools on the team's existing code-search MCP server.", "why": "Works, but still custom code. There is no new server, yet the team writes and maintains Jira code that already exists, inside a server built for something else.", "trap": "still custom code"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "These are ordinary Jira tasks.", "explanation": "For standard integrations such as Jira, prefer an existing community MCP server over writing a custom one.", "eli5": {"question": "The team needs Claude to read and comment on Jira tickets, and someone wants to build a connector from scratch.", "answer": "If you need a kettle, you buy a good one from the shop; you don't build one from parts. Jira is the kettle: lots of teams need the same thing, so a well-tested <b>community MCP server</b> already exists. Build your own only for systems nobody else has. <b>Rule: standard integration → community server; your own system → your own server.</b>", "options": ["A hand-built kettle works, but you'll be fixing it for years.", "Boiling water over a campfire works, but it's clumsy every time.", "Correct. Buy the good kettle everyone uses, and put it in the shared kitchen.", "Bolting a hand-built kettle onto the toaster: still yours to fix."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a custom server is the careful choice</b> because you control it. For standard integrations the exam guide says the opposite: prefer a community MCP server. Custom code is for your own systems, where nothing exists to adopt.", "mapTitle": "Adopt, build, or work around?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best choice", "Why"], "rows": [["Standard service (Jira, GitHub)", "Community MCP server", "Already built and maintained"], ["Your own internal system", "Your own MCP server", "Nothing exists to adopt"], ["The team and CI need it", "Configure it in project <code>.mcp.json</code>", "Shared on clone"], ["Only you, trying it out", "User-level <code>~/.claude.json</code>", "Personal"], ["Claude explores to learn what exists", "Add an MCP resource", "A catalog cuts exploratory calls"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The review job uses an MCP server for code search and API guidelines", "means": "The team already works through MCP; adding another server fits."}, {"quote": "It also needs to search related tickets and add a comment", "means": "It must act, not only read, so it needs real Jira tools."}, {"quote": "These are ordinary Jira tasks", "means": "The decider: a standard integration, so a community server exists."}, {"quote": "A developer plans to write the team's own MCP server for them", "means": "Building it (<b>[[0]]</b>), bolting it onto another server (<b>[[3]]</b>) or scripting around it (<b>[[1]]</b>) is more to maintain. Adopt: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Standard integration (Jira)? Configure a community MCP server; build your own only for your own systems.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "prefer community MCP servers for standard integrations (Jira)"}}}, {"id": "tg-mcp-where-what-3", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 MCP resources for schema catalogs", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Engineers ask Claude questions about data in the staging database. Before each answer, Claude runs many queries on the system tables to learn which tables and columns exist. Most of those queries find nothing useful. The database has several hundred tables, and its structure changes only when a migration runs. What is the <b>best way</b> to cut these exploratory calls?", "options": [{"t": "Add a <code>describe_table</code> tool that returns the columns of one named table.", "why": "Works, but still exploring. Claude must already know a table's name, so it still calls once per guess.", "trap": "still exploratory"}, {"t": "Paste the full schema into the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> for every session to load.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. Hundreds of tables load into every session, even ones about other work, and the copy drifts after each migration.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Expand the query tool's description with sample queries on the system tables.", "why": "Works, but a different problem. The queries get better aimed, but Claude still runs them to explore.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Expose the schema as an MCP resource: tables, columns and a summary of each.", "why": "Best. A resource is a catalog Claude reads when it needs it. One read shows what exists, so the exploratory queries go away."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Claude runs many queries on the system tables to learn which tables and columns exist.", "explanation": "MCP resources expose content catalogs, such as schemas, so the agent can see what exists without exploratory tool calls.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude keeps knocking on doors to find out which rooms the building has.", "answer": "Give it the floor plan instead. A floor plan on the wall shows every room at a glance, and you only walk to the room you need. An <b>MCP resource</b> is that floor plan: a catalog Claude can read. Tools are the doors you open to <i>do</i> something. <b>Rule: Claude explores to learn what exists → publish a resource.</b>", "options": ["A door you can open only if you know the room's name: still guessing.", "Printing the whole floor plan on everyone's desk every day, even when they work elsewhere.", "Better knocking technique, but still knocking.", "Correct. One floor plan, read once, and no more knocking."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>every MCP feature is a tool</b>, so the fix must be a better or new tool. MCP has a second primitive: a <b>resource</b>, content the client can read, such as a schema or a document list. It answers “what exists?” in one read, which is exactly what the exploratory queries were doing.", "diagram": "Today:  query, query, query … (most find nothing)\nAfter:  read schema resource → one targeted query", "mapTitle": "Tool, resource, or memory?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "MCP primitive", "Why"], "rows": [["Learn what exists (schemas, doc lists)", "Resource", "A catalog read once"], ["Do something with inputs (run a query)", "Tool", "An action with parameters"], ["Claude picks Grep over a better tool", "Better tool description", "Descriptions drive selection"], ["A fact every session needs, short and stable", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Always loaded"], ["Standard service such as Jira", "Community server", "Adopt, don't build"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Claude runs many queries on the system tables to learn which tables and columns exist", "means": "The decider: the calls are exploration. Better-aimed queries (<b>[[2]]</b>) are still exploration."}, {"quote": "Most of those queries find nothing useful", "means": "Wasted calls, not wrong answers. The fix is to make the calls unnecessary."}, {"quote": "The database has several hundred tables", "means": "Too big for always-loaded memory (<b>[[1]]</b>), and too many to guess one by one (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "What is the best way to cut these exploratory calls?", "means": "A catalog Claude reads when needed: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Claude explores to learn what exists? Publish an MCP resource (a catalog); keep tools for actions.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "MCP resources expose content catalogs (issue summaries, doc hierarchies, schemas) to reduce exploratory tool calls."}}}, {"id": "tg-plan-or-do-1", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.4 Direct execution for a well-scoped change", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "A ticket asks for one new check on the sign-up endpoint in <code>server/</code>. Passwords shorter than twelve characters must be rejected with the team's usual error response. The engineer knows the endpoint file and its test file, and the error format already exists. How should the engineer <b>approach</b> this with Claude?", "options": [{"t": "Enter plan mode, have Claude survey the auth code, then approve a written plan.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The plan would say what the ticket already says. Plan mode is for open design choices across many files, and here there are none.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Send an Explore subagent to map every input check in <code>server/</code>, then edit.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The Explore subagent keeps long discovery output out of the session. Here there is nothing to discover: the engineer already knows both files.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Ask Claude to add the check and a test case directly, then run the tests.", "why": "Best. One rule, two known files, an existing error format. A simple, well-scoped change like this is what direct execution is for."}, {"t": "Have Claude interview the engineer about password rules before writing code.", "why": "Works, but nothing is left to ask. The interview pattern draws out unclear requirements. The ticket already states the rule and the error.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The engineer knows the endpoint file and its test file, and the error format already exists.", "explanation": "Direct execution fits simple, well-scoped changes such as a single validation check. Plan mode fits large changes with several valid approaches or architectural decisions.", "eli5": {"question": "A small, clear ticket: add one password check to one endpoint. The clue: the engineer already knows which files to change, and the error format exists.", "answer": "Hanging one picture doesn't need an architect. You know the wall, you have the nail, you hang it. <b>Direct execution</b> is hanging the picture: Claude makes the change and runs the tests. <b>Rule: small and settled → just do it. Big or undecided → plan first.</b>", "options": ["Drawing up a plan for one picture: correct in the end, but slow for no gain.", "Sending someone to measure every wall when you already know which wall.", "Correct. Make the change, add a test, run the tests.", "Asking questions when the ticket has already answered them."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>planning first is always the safer choice</b>. Plan mode costs time: Claude explores, writes a plan, and waits for approval. That pays off when the approach is open or many files change. For one clear rule in two known files, the plan adds time and changes nothing.", "diagram": "known files + settled rule\n  -> edit endpoint + add test case\n  -> run tests -> done", "mapTitle": "Which mode fits the task?", "map": {"head": ["Task looks like", "Mode", "Why"], "rows": [["One rule, known files, settled approach", "Direct execution", "Nothing to decide, so just do it"], ["Many files and two valid approaches", "Plan mode", "Agree the design before any edits"], ["Discovery output would flood the session", "Explore subagent", "Returns a summary, not the raw output"], ["Requirements are unclear", "Interview pattern", "Claude asks questions first"], ["Known right answers, fixes keep breaking", "Tests first, share failures", "Each round has a clear target"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "A ticket asks for one new check on the sign-up endpoint", "means": "One rule in one place: a small, well-scoped change."}, {"quote": "The engineer knows the endpoint file and its test file, and the error format already exists", "means": "The decider. There is nothing to explore (<b>[[1]]</b>) and no design to agree (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Passwords shorter than twelve characters must be rejected with the team's usual error response", "means": "The requirement is already stated, so an interview (<b>[[3]]</b>) has nothing to ask."}, {"quote": "How should the engineer approach this with Claude?", "means": "Make the change and test it: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Small, well-scoped and settled? Direct execution. Plan mode is for big or undecided changes.", "guide": {"obj": "3.4 Plan mode vs direct execution", "quote": "Direct execution: simple well-scoped changes (single validation check)."}}}, {"id": "tg-plan-or-do-2", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.4 Explore subagent for verbose discovery", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer is in plan mode with Claude, designing how to split the large <code>User</code> model into smaller pieces. The design depends on every place in the app that reads or writes user fields. Last time, those searches filled most of the session's context. The design talk that followed lost track of earlier decisions. What should the engineer <b>do</b> for the search?", "options": [{"t": "Have an Explore subagent run the searches and return a summary per field.", "why": "Best. The subagent does the noisy searching in its own context and hands back only the summary. The planning session keeps its room for the design."}, {"t": "Let Claude run the searches in the session, then use <code>/compact</code> before designing.", "why": "Works, but after the damage. Compacting frees space, but the raw output already crowded the session, and the summary it keeps is not chosen for this design.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Leave plan mode and change the model in small steps, searching as each step needs.", "why": "Works, but wrong mode. Splitting a central model is an architectural change. Without a plan, early steps pick a design by default, and later steps redo them.", "trap": "wrong mode"}, {"t": "Save the full usage list in <code>CLAUDE.md</code> for every later session to load.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. The list is loaded into every session, for every task, and goes stale as soon as the code changes.", "trap": "wrong home"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Last time, those searches filled most of the session's context.", "explanation": "The Explore subagent isolates verbose discovery output and returns a summary, so the main session keeps its context for planning and decisions.", "eli5": {"question": "Planning a big change needs a long search through the app, and last time the search output crowded out the planning. The clue: the searches filled the session's context.", "answer": "When you plan a kitchen, you send someone to measure every room. They come back with one sheet of numbers, not a pile of tape-measure notes. The <b>Explore subagent</b> is that helper: it does the messy searching elsewhere and hands back a short summary. <b>Rule: noisy discovery goes to the Explore subagent; the planning session gets the summary.</b>", "options": ["Correct. The helper measures; you get one tidy sheet.", "Letting the notes pile up on the table, then tidying: the mess already got in the way.", "Starting to knock down walls before the plan is agreed.", "Pinning the notes to the fridge for every future job, where they go out of date."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a full context can be fixed afterwards</b>. Compacting helps later, but the design talk already ran in a crowded session. The Explore subagent keeps the long output out of the session from the start. Only its summary comes back.", "diagram": "plan session -> Explore subagent: find user-field usages\n               (long output stays in the subagent)\nplan session <- summary per field", "mapTitle": "Where should long output go?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Tool", "Effect on the main session"], "rows": [["Long discovery before a design", "Explore subagent", "Only the summary comes back"], ["Session already full of old output", "<code>/compact</code>", "Shrinks what is there"], ["Facts every task needs, every time", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Loaded into every session"], ["Architectural change, open design", "Plan mode", "Design agreed before edits"], ["One small, settled edit", "Direct execution", "No discovery needed"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "An engineer is in plan mode with Claude, designing how to split the large User model", "means": "An architectural change: plan mode is right. Leaving it (<b>[[2]]</b>) trades one problem for another."}, {"quote": "Last time, those searches filled most of the session's context", "means": "The decider: the problem is long discovery output landing in the planning session."}, {"quote": "The design talk that followed lost track of earlier decisions", "means": "Compacting after the search (<b>[[1]]</b>) comes after the damage. Keep the output out from the start."}, {"quote": "What should the engineer do for the search?", "means": "Run it in an Explore subagent and take back a summary: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Long discovery output? Give the search to an Explore subagent and keep only its summary.", "guide": {"obj": "3.4 Plan mode vs direct execution", "quote": "Explore subagent isolates verbose discovery output, returns summaries."}}}, {"id": "tg-plan-or-do-3", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.5 Test-driven iteration", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "Claude wrote a function in <code>packages/shared/</code> that prices a booking from its start and end times. Prices come out wrong around midnight and around daylight-saving changes. Each fix, asked for in prose, breaks another case. The team has a list of about twenty such bookings with the correct price for each. How should the engineer <b>get</b> the function right?", "options": [{"t": "Describe the midnight and daylight-saving rules in more detail, and ask for a rewrite.", "why": "Works, but more of the same. Better prose helps a little, but prose is what keeps breaking other cases. Nothing checks every case each round.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Paste three of the bookings into the prompt as input and expected-price examples.", "why": "Works, but covers only some cases. Concrete examples beat prose, but three of twenty leaves the rest unchecked, and nothing runs them after each change.", "trap": "covers only some cases"}, {"t": "Enter plan mode and have Claude design a new pricing approach before any edits.", "why": "Works, but different problem. The approach is not in question; the edge cases are. A new design still needs checking against the same bookings.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Turn the list into tests first, have Claude run them, and share the failures.", "why": "Best. Every known case is checked on every round, and each failure tells Claude exactly what is still wrong. Fixes can't quietly break another case."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The team has a list of about twenty such bookings with the correct price for each.", "explanation": "When the right answers are known, write them as tests first and share the failures with Claude. Test-driven iteration gives each round a clear target and catches fixes that break other cases.", "eli5": {"question": "A pricing function keeps getting edge cases wrong, and each fix breaks another. The clue: the team already knows the right price for twenty tricky bookings.", "answer": "If a student keeps getting sums wrong, you don't explain the rules again in longer words. You give them the answer sheet as a checklist, and after each try you show which sums are still wrong. <b>Tests first, then share the failures</b> is that checklist. <b>Rule: known right answers → tests first, and feed Claude the failures.</b>", "options": ["Explaining the rules again in longer words.", "Showing three worked sums out of twenty, and never checking the rest.", "Redesigning the method when the problem is a handful of tricky sums.", "Correct. Mark every sum after every try, and show what is still wrong."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a clearer description will finally get it right</b>. The description is what keeps failing: each prose fix breaks another case. When the right answers are known, turn them into tests. Every round is then checked against every case, and the failures say exactly what to fix.", "diagram": "write tests from the twenty bookings\n  -> Claude edits -> run tests\n  -> share failures -> repeat until all pass", "mapTitle": "How do you tell Claude what “right” means?", "map": {"head": ["You have", "Technique", "What it gives Claude"], "rows": [["Known right answers for many cases", "Tests first, share failures", "A target checked every round"], ["A few clear before/after examples", "Input and output examples", "The shape of the change"], ["Unclear requirements", "Interview pattern", "Questions answered before code"], ["Several problems that affect each other", "One message with all of them", "Fixes made together"], ["Independent problems", "One at a time, in sequence", "Clean, separate fixes"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each fix, asked for in prose, breaks another case", "means": "More prose (<b>[[0]]</b>) repeats what is failing."}, {"quote": "The team has a list of about twenty such bookings with the correct price for each", "means": "The decider: the right answers are known, so they can become tests. Three examples (<b>[[1]]</b>) leave most of them unchecked."}, {"quote": "Prices come out wrong around midnight and around daylight-saving changes", "means": "Edge cases, not an open design question, so plan mode (<b>[[2]]</b>) aims at the wrong thing."}, {"quote": "How should the engineer get the function right?", "means": "Tests first, then share the failures each round: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Know the right answers? Write them as tests first, then share the failures with Claude.", "guide": {"obj": "3.5 Iterative refinement techniques", "quote": "Test-driven iteration (write tests first, share failures)"}}}, {"id": "m7p-s1-04", "was": "m7-s1-04", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.5 PostToolUse hooks for transformation", "trap": "Prompt where enforcement was required", "fam": 1, "select": 1, "question": "<code>get_customer</code> returns the full card number on file, and the team that owns that server will not change it. Twice, the agent read a card number to a customer. Security says this must never happen. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a system-prompt rule to mention cards by their last four digits only, with two examples.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A clear rule with examples cuts the leaks, but prompts have a non-zero failure rate, and this is a must-never.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that masks the card field in results before Claude reads them.", "why": "Best. The hook changes the result before Claude reads it, so the agent can't repeat a number it never saw. The server stays as it is."}, {"t": "Route <code>get_customer</code> through a subagent told to pass on only the name, plan and box IDs.", "why": "Works, but still a model choosing. The subagent sees the full number and decides what to pass on; its instructions are not a filter.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Scan each reply for card-number patterns and block the reply before it reaches the customer.", "why": "Works, but only for the formats it knows. A number read out with spaces or in parts slips past, and Claude still has the number.", "trap": "catches only some cases"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Security says this must never happen.", "explanation": "When a tool returns data the model must never use, change the result with a PostToolUse hook before the model sees it. Prompt rules only make misuse less likely.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent sometimes reads a customer's card number out loud. The clue: security says this must <i>never</i> happen.", "answer": "If a receptionist must never read out card numbers, don't hand them the number: black it out on the paper first. A <b>PostToolUse hook</b> is that black marker. It edits each tool result before Claude reads it, and Claude can't repeat what it never saw. <b>Rule: if the model must never use some data, remove it before the model sees it.</b>", "options": ["A written rule usually works, but “usually” isn't enough for a must-never.", "Correct. Black out the number before Claude ever sees it.", "A helper told to pass on only some fields still sees the number, and might pass it on.", "Checking each reply catches the obvious cases, but Claude still knows the number."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a firm instruction stops the model saying something</b>. It lowers the chance, but prompt compliance is probabilistic. For data that must never reach a customer, the reliable place to act is the tool result itself: change it before the model reads it.", "diagram": "get_customer → [PostToolUse: mask card] → Claude\nserver unchanged        model never sees the number", "mapTitle": "Which hook, and where does it act?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "Mechanism", "Where it acts"], "rows": [["Model must never see or repeat a field", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook masks it", "On the result, before Claude reads it"], ["An action must never run in some case", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook denies the call", "On the call, before the tool runs"], ["Messy result formats (dates, codes)", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook normalises them", "On the result"], ["A wording or tone preference", "Prompt instruction + examples", "In Claude's instructions"], ["You own the tool and nobody needs the field", "Stop returning it", "In the tool itself"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "get_customer returns the full card number on file, and the team that owns that server will not change it", "means": "The fix has to happen on the agent's side, between the tool and Claude."}, {"quote": "Twice, the agent read a card number to a customer", "means": "The number reaches Claude in ordinary chats. Scanning replies (<b>[[3]]</b>) catches only the patterns it knows."}, {"quote": "Security says this must never happen", "means": "The decider: a must-never. A prompt rule (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a subagent told what to pass on (<b>[[2]]</b>) both still rely on the model choosing."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Change the result before Claude sees it: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Model must never use some data? Strip it from the tool result with a PostToolUse hook.", "guide": {"obj": "1.5 Apply Agent SDK hooks for tool call interception and data normalization", "quote": "PostToolUse hooks intercept results for transformation before the model sees them."}}}, {"id": "m7p-s1-11", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.5 Hooks that block outgoing tool calls", "trap": "Prompt where enforcement was required", "fam": 1, "select": 1, "question": "Refunds above $150 must go to a human. The system prompt says so, yet a monthly review found a few larger refunds that the agent made itself. Finance wants this to stop completely. What should the team <b>do</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks <code>process_refund</code> over the limit and hands the case to a human.", "why": "Best. The hook checks every refund call before it runs. Code enforces the limit every time, which a prompt can't promise."}, {"t": "Restate the limit first in Claude's instructions, with three examples of declining big refunds.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Clearer prompting cuts the misses but can't remove them, and finance wants zero.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Run a nightly job that finds refunds over $150, reverses them and sends each case to a human.", "why": "Works, but after the money has gone. Reversals catch every case, but customers see a refund taken back, and the rule was still broken first.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Have a second Claude instance review each refund decision before the agent replies to the customer.", "why": "Works, but still a model's judgement. A second instance catches some cases but is probabilistic too, and it adds a model call to every refund.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Finance wants this to stop completely.", "explanation": "Business rules that must hold every time are enforced in code. A PreToolUse hook can inspect each outgoing tool call and block the ones that break the rule.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent was told “no refunds over $150”, and still made a few. The clue: finance wants <i>zero</i>.", "answer": "Telling a cashier “never give more than $150 back” works most days. A till that won't open above $150 without a manager's key works every day. A <b>PreToolUse hook</b> is that till: it checks the refund before it happens. <b>Rule: must hold every time → check it in code, before the action runs.</b>", "options": ["Correct. The till won't open above the limit.", "A bigger sign by the till still relies on the cashier remembering.", "Taking money back the next day is messier than not paying it out.", "A second cashier watching helps, but can also miss things, and costs more."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>clearer instructions will finish the job</b>. They reduce the misses, but a prompt is followed most of the time, not every time. When the requirement is “never”, put a check in code on the tool call itself, before the action runs.", "diagram": "Claude → process_refund($180)\n          → [PreToolUse: over $150? deny + escalate]\n          → tool never runs", "mapTitle": "Before, after, or in the prompt?", "map": {"head": ["Requirement", "Mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["An action must never run in some case", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook denies the call", "Checks before anything happens"], ["A step must come first (verify, then refund)", "Prerequisite gate in code", "Blocks the later tool until the first succeeds"], ["Data in a result must not reach Claude", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook masks it", "Acts on the result"], ["Behaviour should usually follow a style", "Prompt + examples", "Probabilistic is fine"], ["Output needs a second opinion on quality", "Independent review instance", "Fresh eyes, still probabilistic"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Refunds above $150 must go to a human", "means": "A hard business rule tied to one tool call, which a hook can check."}, {"quote": "The system prompt says so, yet a monthly review found a few larger refunds", "means": "The prompt already tried. Moving or repeating it (<b>[[1]]</b>) only lowers the rate."}, {"quote": "Finance wants this to stop completely", "means": "The decider: zero misses means code, before the action. A reviewer model (<b>[[3]]</b>) is probabilistic, and a nightly reversal (<b>[[2]]</b>) comes after the refund."}, {"quote": "What should the team do?", "means": "Check every refund call before it runs: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Must never happen? Block the tool call in a PreToolUse hook, before the action runs.", "guide": {"obj": "1.5 Apply Agent SDK hooks for tool call interception and data normalization", "quote": "Hooks intercept outgoing tool calls to enforce compliance (e.g., block refunds above a threshold)."}}}, {"id": "tg-tool-failure-1", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.3 Report access failures with partial results", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "The document-analysis subagent fetches each paper's full text through the journals' MCP server. Some papers sit behind paywalls the hospital does not subscribe to, and the server refuses them. The subagent analyses the rest and returns only those findings. Summaries then read as if no other evidence exists. How should the subagent <b>report this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Return its findings plus a structured error naming each refused paper and the reason.", "why": "Best. The coordinator gets the work that succeeded and knows exactly what is missing and why, so it can ask the library, try another source or publish with the gap stated."}, {"t": "Retry refused papers with backoff, then return the findings and “some papers unavailable”.", "why": "Works, but it hides the detail. A paywall refusal won't change on retry, and “some papers” doesn't say which ones or why.", "trap": "hides the context"}, {"t": "Stop with an error until every paper can be fetched, and return no findings before then.", "why": "Works, but it throws away good work. One refused paper shouldn't end a run whose other analyses are finished and useful.", "trap": "ends the run"}, {"t": "Return its findings, and add a line to each summary that some sources may be missing.", "why": "Works, but vaguely. The committee is warned, yet nobody knows which papers are missing, so nobody can fetch them or judge how much they matter.", "trap": "loses which ones"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "Summaries then read as if no other evidence exists.", "explanation": "Report failures with structured context: what failed, why, what was tried and the partial results. Silently returning only the successes makes a gap look like a finding.", "eli5": {"question": "Some papers couldn't be opened, and the report pretends they don't exist. The clue: the summaries read as if there's no other evidence.", "answer": "If an assistant can't get into three of the files, you want to hear “here are 17, and these 3 were locked”, not just the 17. Then you can ask for a key. <b>Report what worked and exactly what didn't, with the reason.</b> <b>Rule: never let a failure look like an empty result.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Here's what I have, and here's exactly what's locked.", "Knocking on a locked door again won't open it, and “some files” doesn't say which.", "Refusing to hand over anything because three files were locked wastes the 17.", "“Some may be missing” worries people without telling them what to fetch."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>returning only what worked is the clean, safe option</b>. But silence about a failure reads as “nothing else exists”. The coordinator can only recover from a failure it can see: what failed, why, and what is already done.", "diagram": "server refuses paywalled papers\ntoday:  findings only -> gap looks like no evidence\nbetter: findings + refused list + reason\n        -> coordinator decides", "mapTitle": "How the failure reaches the coordinator", "map": {"head": ["Report", "What the coordinator knows", "Outcome"], "rows": [["Findings only", "Thinks the set is complete", "Gap reads as “no evidence”"], ["Findings + “some missing”", "Something is missing, not what", "Can't fetch or weigh the gap"], ["Exception, run ends", "Nothing; finished work lost", "Committee gets nothing"], ["Findings + structured failure", "Which papers, why, no retry", "Ask library, other source, or state the gap"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the server refuses them", "means": "An access failure, not an empty result. A paywall refusal won't change on retry (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "The subagent analyses the rest and returns only those findings", "means": "The failure is dropped on the way up. Stopping the run (<b>[[2]]</b>) would swing too far the other way."}, {"quote": "Summaries then read as if no other evidence exists", "means": "The decider: lost information has turned into a false impression. A vague warning (<b>[[3]]</b>) doesn't say what to fetch."}, {"quote": "every claim must cite a paper and section", "means": "The committee needs to know which papers were never read, by name."}], "rule": "Report a failure with its type, what was tried and the partial results. Silence reads as “no evidence”.", "guide": {"obj": "5.3 Error propagation across multi-agent systems", "quote": "Structured error context (failure type, attempted query, partial results, alternatives) enables coordinator recovery."}}}, {"id": "tg-tool-failure-2", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.2 Access failure vs valid empty result", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "The extraction agent calls a <code>verify_carrier</code> MCP tool to check each carrier against the licensing registry. The registry sometimes goes offline for an hour or more. During those outages the tool returns “Carrier not found”, the same text it returns for a carrier with no licence. Certificates from licensed carriers are then rejected as unlicensed. Which change is <b>best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Retry the registry with backoff inside the tool before it returns “Carrier not found”.", "why": "Works, but only for short blips. After an hour-long outage the retries run out and the tool still says “not found”, a wrong answer that looks like a real one.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Send each “Carrier not found” certificate to a reviewer before it is rejected.", "why": "Works, but it treats the symptom. Reviewers catch the wrong rejections, at the cost of checking every real miss too, while the tool keeps saying something false.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Answer from a cache of earlier registry lookups while the registry is offline.", "why": "Works, but only for carriers seen before, and the cached answer may be out of date. A new carrier still comes back “not found”.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Return a transient, retryable <code>isError</code> result when the registry is offline.", "why": "Best. The agent can now tell “the registry couldn't be reached” from “the registry says no licence”, and hold the certificate until the lookup works."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "the same text it returns for a carrier with no licence", "explanation": "A tool must distinguish an access failure from a valid empty result. Return an isError result with an error category and a retryable flag, and keep “not found” for lookups that actually worked.", "eli5": {"question": "When the registry is down, the tool says “not found”, the same words it uses for a real “no licence”. The clue: two very different events give the same answer.", "answer": "If you phone a shop and nobody picks up, that doesn't mean the shop doesn't sell bread. “No answer” and “we have none” are different news. A tool must say which one happened: an <b>isError</b> result marked <b>transient, retryable</b> for “couldn't reach it”. <b>Rule: never report an access failure as an empty result.</b>", "options": ["Ringing again helps if the line is busy for a minute, not for an hour.", "Asking a person to double-check every “none” works, but the phone is still lying.", "Remembering last week's answer helps only for shops you've rung before.", "Correct. Say “nobody answered”, not “they have none”."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>“not found” is a safe default when a lookup fails</b>. It isn't: it turns “we don't know” into a confident wrong answer. Downstream logic acts on it as fact. The tool has to report the failure as a failure.", "diagram": "offline -> \"Carrier not found\" -> rejected (wrong)\noffline -> isError, transient, retryable\n        -> hold, retry later", "mapTitle": "What the tool returns, and what the agent can do", "map": {"head": ["What happened", "What the tool returns", "What the agent can do"], "rows": [["Registry answered: no licence", "Success, “not found”", "Reject as unlicensed"], ["Registry offline", "<code>isError</code>, transient, retryable", "Hold and retry later"], ["Bad input (malformed carrier ID)", "<code>isError</code>, validation", "Fix the input and call again"], ["Registry offline, reported as “not found”", "A false empty result", "Rejects good certificates"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The registry sometimes goes offline for an hour or more", "means": "Too long for retries inside the tool (<b>[[0]]</b>) to wait out."}, {"quote": "the same text it returns for a carrier with no licence", "means": "The decider: an access failure and a valid empty result look identical. Only the tool can tell them apart."}, {"quote": "Certificates from licensed carriers are then rejected as unlicensed", "means": "Wrong answers acted on as fact. A reviewer (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a cache (<b>[[2]]</b>) works around the false answer instead of removing it."}, {"quote": "Which change is best?", "means": "Report the outage as an error with its category: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Couldn't reach it is not the same as found nothing. Return isError with a category and a retryable flag.", "guide": {"obj": "2.2 Implement structured error responses for MCP tools", "quote": "distinguish access failures from valid empty results."}}}, {"id": "tg-tool-failure-3", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.3 Local recovery, propagate only unresolved errors", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "The weekly dependency-risk report runs one subagent per module. Each subagent looks up every package in an advisory database over MCP. A few lookups per run time out, and they succeed on a second try seconds later. Today each timeout goes straight to the coordinator, which restarts that module's scan from the start. Runs now take most of the night. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have the coordinator wait a minute before it restarts a module after a timeout.", "why": "Works, but it still throws away finished lookups. Each restart redoes the whole module for one failed package.", "trap": "redoes finished work"}, {"t": "Have the subagent retry blips itself and pass up only lookups that still fail.", "why": "Best. Short blips are fixed where they happen, finished lookups are kept, and the coordinator hears only about failures it has to decide on."}, {"t": "Raise the advisory client's timeout to give slow lookups longer to finish.", "why": "Works, but it hides the problem. Fewer timeouts, yet a hung call now holds the run for longer, and any timeout that remains still restarts the module.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Have the subagent skip timed-out packages and list them in the report as not checked.", "why": "Works, but it gives up too soon. A lookup that would succeed seconds later is left as a gap in the report every week.", "trap": "gives up too soon"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "they succeed on a second try seconds later", "explanation": "Recover locally from transient errors in the subagent, and propagate only unresolved errors, with the partial results, to the coordinator.", "eli5": {"question": "A few lookups hiccup and work on a second try, but each hiccup restarts a whole module. The clue: a second try seconds later works.", "answer": "If one page of a 40-page print job jams, the printer reprints that page, not the whole document, and only calls you if it keeps jamming. A subagent should do the same: <b>retry the blip itself</b>, keep what's done, and <b>report up only what is still broken</b>. <b>Rule: recover locally, propagate only unresolved errors.</b>", "options": ["Waiting before reprinting the whole document still reprints the whole document.", "Correct. Reprint the jammed page; call for help only if it keeps jamming.", "A longer wait for each page means a stuck page holds everything up longer.", "Leaving the jammed page out works, but the document has a hole every week."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>every error should go straight to the coordinator, so nothing is hidden</b>. But a blip that a quick retry fixes isn't a decision for the coordinator. Handle transient errors locally; send up only what is still unresolved, together with the partial results.", "diagram": "timeout -> subagent retries (seconds) -> ok\nstill failing -> error + results so far\n              -> coordinator", "mapTitle": "Who handles which failure", "map": {"head": ["Failure", "Who handles it", "How"], "rows": [["Brief timeout, fine on retry", "The subagent", "Retry locally, keep the results"], ["Still failing after retries", "The coordinator", "Structured error + partial results"], ["Business or permission refusal", "The coordinator", "Not retryable; report the reason"], ["Bad input to the tool", "The subagent", "Fix the input and call again"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "they succeed on a second try seconds later", "means": "The decider: these are transient blips that a local retry fixes. Skipping them (<b>[[3]]</b>) gives up on lookups that would work."}, {"quote": "Today each timeout goes straight to the coordinator, which restarts that module's scan from the start", "means": "Finished lookups are thrown away. Waiting first (<b>[[0]]</b>) still restarts the module."}, {"quote": "Runs now take most of the night", "means": "The cost of handling every blip at the top. A longer timeout (<b>[[2]]</b>) trades restarts for longer waits."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Retry in the subagent, propagate only what is still failing: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Recover from blips inside the subagent; send up only unresolved errors, with partial results.", "guide": {"obj": "2.2 Implement structured error responses for MCP tools", "quote": "local recovery in subagents, propagate only unresolved errors with partial results"}}}, {"id": "tg-tool-picker-1", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.1 Descriptions that separate similar tools", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "The search subagent has two MCP tools, <code>search_journals</code> and <code>search_trial_registry</code>. Both descriptions read “Searches for studies on a drug.” Committees often ask about trials that are still running. The subagent sends these questions to <code>search_journals</code>, which holds only published papers, and reports that nothing was found. What is the most effective <b>first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a routing step that sends questions about ongoing or recruiting trials to the registry tool.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Keyword code catches the phrasings it was written for and misses the rest, and it has to be kept up as questions change.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Rewrite both descriptions: what each source holds, example queries, and when to use the other.", "why": "Best. Descriptions are what Claude reads to choose a tool. Saying what each one covers, with examples and a boundary, fixes the cause in two edits."}, {"t": "Merge the two tools into one <code>search_studies</code> tool that queries both sources at once.", "why": "Works, but more than a first step. Every question now pays for two searches, and the server and its callers have to change.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add five example questions to the subagent's prompt, each showing which search tool to call.", "why": "Works, but misses the cause. Examples help on questions like the ones shown, add tokens to every call, and leave two identical descriptions in place.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "Both descriptions read “Searches for studies on a drug.”", "explanation": "Tool descriptions are the primary way Claude chooses between tools. When two tools share a vague description, the first step is to say what each one covers, with input formats, example queries and boundaries.", "eli5": {"question": "Two drawers carry the same label, “Studies”. One holds published papers; the other holds trials still running. The helper keeps opening the wrong one.", "answer": "Before you hire a sorter or knock the drawers together, <b>write better labels</b>: “Published papers: use for finished studies” and “Trials still running: use for ongoing or recruiting trials”. Claude picks tools by their descriptions the way you pick drawers by their labels. <b>Rule: similar tools, wrong picks → fix the descriptions first.</b>", "options": ["A sorter at the door helps, but only for the words it was told to look for.", "Correct. Clear labels on both drawers.", "One big drawer works, but you search everything every time and have to rebuild the cabinet.", "Showing a few examples helps, but the labels still say the same thing."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the model needs extra machinery to choose</b>: a router, a merged tool, or examples. Claude chooses a tool mainly from its description. Two tools with the same one-line description give it nothing to choose by, so the first fix is the descriptions themselves.", "diagram": "question → Claude reads descriptions → picks a tool\n\"Searches for studies on a drug\" x2 → coin toss\nsay what each holds + when to use the other → right pick", "mapTitle": "What fixes a wrong tool choice?", "map": {"head": ["Cause", "Fix", "Size"], "rows": [["Similar tools, vague descriptions", "Formats, examples, edge cases, boundaries in each description", "Small, first step"], ["Descriptions are clear, choices still inconsistent", "A few targeted examples in the prompt", "Small, later step"], ["Too many tools for one agent", "Give the agent only the tools its role needs", "Medium"], ["One generic tool doing several jobs", "Split it into purpose-specific tools", "Medium"], ["Picking by keywords in code", "Routing layer", "Large; usually overbuilt"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Both descriptions read “Searches for studies on a drug.”", "means": "The decider: Claude has nothing to tell the two tools apart. That is the cause, and examples in the prompt (<b>[[3]]</b>) work around it."}, {"quote": "which holds only published papers", "means": "A boundary the description should state: running trials belong in the registry."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective first step?", "means": "The cheapest change that fixes the cause: <b>[[1]]</b>. A router (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a merged tool (<b>[[2]]</b>) are bigger builds."}], "rule": "Claude picks the wrong one of two similar tools? Fix the descriptions first.", "guide": {"obj": "2.1 Design effective tool interfaces with clear descriptions and boundaries", "quote": "Include input formats, example queries, edge cases, boundaries."}}}, {"id": "tg-tool-picker-2", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.1 Keyword wording that pulls the wrong tool", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "The system prompt says: “When a customer mentions stopping deliveries, use <code>pause_subscription</code>.” Company policy is that cancellations go to a person through <code>escalate_to_human</code>. Customers who write “stop sending boxes, I want to cancel” now get a paused plan instead. They are billed again when the pause ends. The <code>pause_subscription</code> description says only “Pauses deliveries.” What is the best <b>fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep the line and add three example chats where cancelling customers are sent to a person.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The examples pull one way and the keyword line still pulls the other, so some cancellations keep getting paused.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks a pause when the message mentions cancelling.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Code now guesses intent from words, and it blocks customers who say “don't cancel, just pause”.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add a <code>cancel_request</code> tool that hands cancellations to staff, described for that case.", "why": "Works, but a new tool for a job <code>escalate_to_human</code> already does. Now three tools overlap instead of two.", "trap": "new tool"}, {"t": "Drop the keyword line, and say in each tool's description which requests it is for.", "why": "Best. The line ties a word to a tool, which is what causes the wrong pick. Clear descriptions with boundaries let Claude choose by what the customer wants."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "When a customer mentions stopping deliveries, use pause_subscription.", "explanation": "Keyword-sensitive system-prompt wording creates unintended tool associations. Remove the association and put each tool's purpose and boundaries in its description.", "eli5": {"question": "A sign says “Anyone who says <i>stop</i>, send to the pause desk.” So people who say “stop, I'm leaving” end up paused, not cancelled.", "answer": "Take the sign down, and label each desk by what it does: “Pause desk: a break from deliveries. Leaving for good? Go to the person at the front.” <b>Rule: a prompt line that ties a word to a tool causes misrouting → remove it and put the boundary in the tool descriptions.</b>", "options": ["Showing people the right desk helps, but the sign still points the wrong way.", "A guard who stops anyone saying “cancel” also stops people who said “don't cancel”.", "A new desk for leavers works, but the front desk already does that job.", "Correct. Remove the misleading sign and label the desks."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a misrouting needs new code or a new tool</b>. Here the system prompt itself causes it: a keyword rule sends any mention of stopping to one tool. Remove the cause, and give each tool a description that says what it is for and what it is not for.", "diagram": "\"stop ... cancel\" → prompt rule: stop = pause → paused\nrule gone + clear descriptions → escalate_to_human", "mapTitle": "Where does the wrong pick come from?", "map": {"head": ["Source of the misroute", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["A prompt line ties a keyword to a tool", "Remove or reword it by intent", "The line causes the pull"], ["Tool descriptions are one line", "Add purpose, examples and boundaries", "Descriptions drive the choice"], ["Two tools do the same job", "Keep one, describe it clearly", "Overlap causes misrouting"], ["An action must never run in some case", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook", "Code for must-hold rules"], ["Clear descriptions, still inconsistent", "A few targeted examples", "A later step"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "When a customer mentions stopping deliveries, use pause_subscription", "means": "The decider: a keyword rule in the prompt. Examples added beside it (<b>[[0]]</b>) still compete with it."}, {"quote": "cancellations go to a person through escalate_to_human", "means": "A tool for this already exists, so a new one (<b>[[2]]</b>) adds overlap."}, {"quote": "The pause_subscription description says only “Pauses deliveries.”", "means": "No boundary to choose by. A hook guessing intent from words (<b>[[1]]</b>) is code for a wording problem."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Remove the keyword line and describe each tool clearly: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "A prompt line ties a word to a tool? Remove it, and put the boundary in the tool descriptions.", "guide": {"obj": "2.1 Design effective tool interfaces with clear descriptions and boundaries", "quote": "Ambiguous/overlapping descriptions cause misrouting; keyword-sensitive system-prompt wording creates unintended tool associations."}}}, {"id": "tg-tool-picker-3", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 Too many tools for one agent", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "An Agent SDK script triages new bug reports in Linear. It was given every tool from both MCP servers, 18 in all, plus the built-in tools. Its job needs about five of them. It often calls <code>update_issue</code> when it should call <code>move_issue</code>. Sometimes it queries staging when no data is involved. Every tool already has a detailed description. What is the best <b>fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Rewrite all the tool descriptions with sharper boundaries between issue and database tools.", "why": "Works, but misses the cause. The descriptions are already detailed; the number of tools is what lowers the choice quality.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Add a classifier that reads each bug report and picks which tools to offer for it.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The script does one job, so its tool set can be fixed once instead of chosen per report.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Give the triage script only the tools its job needs and leave the rest out.", "why": "Best. Fewer tools make the choice more reliable, and a triage script has no reason to reach the database."}, {"t": "Merge <code>update_issue</code> and <code>move_issue</code> into one <code>change_issue</code> tool with a mode field.", "why": "Works, but fixes one pair. The choice moves into a parameter, and the database calls still happen.", "trap": "fixes one symptom"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "It was given every tool from both MCP servers, 18 in all, plus the built-in tools.", "explanation": "Giving an agent many tools degrades selection reliability, and tools outside its role get misused. Scope each agent to the tools its job needs.", "eli5": {"question": "A helper sorting mail has been handed every tool in the building, and keeps grabbing the wrong one.", "answer": "Better labels on 18 tools still leave 18 tools to pick from. Give the mail sorter the five things a mail sorter needs. <b>Rule: wrong picks among many tools, with good descriptions already → cut the agent's tool set to its job.</b>", "options": ["Clearer labels help, but the labels are already good and the pile is still too big.", "A helper who hands out tools for each letter works, but the job never changes.", "Correct. Give the sorter only the tools for sorting.", "Gluing two tools together fixes one mix-up, not the pile."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>every wrong pick is a description problem</b>. Descriptions come first when they are vague. Here they are already detailed, and the agent holds far more tools than its job needs. Too many tools degrade the choice, and tools outside the role get misused.", "diagram": "18 tools + built-ins → triage script → wrong picks\n5 triage tools → triage script → right picks", "mapTitle": "Descriptions, or the tool set?", "map": {"head": ["Symptom", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["Similar tools, one-line descriptions", "Better descriptions", "Primary selection mechanism"], ["Many tools, good descriptions, wrong picks", "Scope the tool set to the role", "Too many tools degrade selection"], ["Agent uses tools outside its job", "Remove them from its definition", "Off-role tools get misused"], ["One role often needs one outside tool", "Give a limited cross-role tool", "Keeps the common case fast"], ["Tool must run first", "Forced <code>tool_choice</code>", "Fixes order, not choice"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It was given every tool from both MCP servers, 18 in all, plus the built-in tools", "means": "The decider: the tool count is the cause."}, {"quote": "Sometimes it queries staging when no data is involved", "means": "A tool outside its job gets misused. Merging two issue tools (<b>[[3]]</b>) leaves this in place."}, {"quote": "Every tool already has a detailed description", "means": "Rewriting them (<b>[[0]]</b>) does not reach the cause."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "A fixed, smaller tool set for this one job: <b>[[2]]</b>. A per-report classifier (<b>[[1]]</b>) is more than a fixed job needs."}], "rule": "Good descriptions and still wrong picks among many tools? Give the agent only the tools its job needs.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Too many tools (18 vs 4-5) degrades selection reliability."}}}, {"id": "tg-who-escalates-1", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.2 Calibrating when to escalate", "trap": "Unreliable proxy", "fam": 6, "select": 1, "question": "The agent's prompt says to “escalate when needed.” Upset customers with simple problems, such as one missing item, are often handed to a person. Calm customers who ask for something policy does not cover are usually handled by the agent alone. In some of those chats it promises refunds that policy does not allow. Which change best improves <b>when the agent escalates</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have the agent rate its confidence in each case and escalate when the rating is low.", "why": "Works, but an unreliable signal. The agent is often most sure of itself on exactly the cases it gets wrong, such as the calm policy exceptions.", "trap": "unreliable proxy"}, {"t": "Train a separate classifier on past tickets to decide which chats go to a person.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It needs labelled history and retraining, and it learns past habits, including the bad ones described here.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Escalate when a sentiment check finds the customer upset, and let the agent handle the rest.", "why": "Works, but the wrong signal. Mood is not complexity: it sends more simple cases to people and still misses the calm exceptions.", "trap": "unreliable proxy"}, {"t": "State escalation criteria in the prompt, with examples of escalate versus resolve.", "why": "Best. The problem is an unclear boundary. Stating the triggers and showing a few cases on each side fixes that directly, at prompt cost."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Calm customers who ask for something policy does not cover are usually handled by the agent alone.", "explanation": "When escalation is miscalibrated, define explicit escalation criteria and show few-shot examples of escalate versus resolve. Sentiment and self-reported confidence are unreliable proxies for complexity.", "eli5": {"question": "A new shop assistant calls the manager whenever a customer frowns, but quietly bends the rules for polite customers.", "answer": "Don't hand them a mood meter or ask how sure they feel. Tell them <b>when to call the manager</b> (“anything the refund policy doesn't cover; anyone who asks for the manager”) and walk through a few real cases. <b>Rule: wrong escalations → clear criteria plus a few examples, not mood or self-confidence.</b>", "options": ["Asking “how sure are you?” fails: they feel surest when they're bending the rules.", "Studying years of old cases works, but it's a big job and copies old mistakes.", "A mood meter calls the manager for frowns, not for hard cases.", "Correct. Say when to call the manager, and show a few cases."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>escalation needs a signal the model can't fake</b>: a mood score, a confidence number, a trained classifier. The real problem is that nobody told the agent where the line is. Upset is not the same as complex, and the model's own confidence is poorly calibrated.", "diagram": "\"escalate when needed\" → mood decides → wrong cases\ncriteria + examples:\n  policy gap? asked for a person? → escalate\n  simple, even if upset → resolve", "mapTitle": "Which signal tells you to escalate?", "map": {"head": ["Signal", "Reliable?", "Why"], "rows": [["Customer asks for a person", "Yes: escalate at once", "An explicit trigger"], ["Policy exception or gap", "Yes: escalate", "Needs a human decision"], ["Agent cannot make progress", "Yes: escalate", "Stuck is a trigger"], ["Customer sounds upset", "No", "Sentiment is not complexity"], ["Agent says it is unsure", "No", "Self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "escalate when needed", "means": "The cause: no criteria. The fix is to write them down and show examples: <b>[[3]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Upset customers with simple problems, such as one missing item, are often handed to a person", "means": "Mood is already driving escalation. A sentiment check (<b>[[2]]</b>) makes that worse."}, {"quote": "Calm customers who ask for something policy does not cover are usually handled by the agent alone", "means": "The decider: the hard cases look calm and feel easy, so neither mood nor self-rated confidence (<b>[[0]]</b>) catches them."}, {"quote": "Which change best improves when the agent escalates?", "means": "The proportionate fix is in the prompt. A classifier (<b>[[1]]</b>) is a bigger build."}], "rule": "Escalating the wrong cases? Explicit criteria plus examples, not sentiment or self-rated confidence.", "guide": {"obj": "5.2 Escalation and ambiguity resolution patterns", "quote": "Sentiment-based escalation and self-reported confidence are unreliable proxies for complexity."}}}, {"id": "tg-who-escalates-2", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Structured handoff summaries", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "When the agent escalates, staff pick up the case from a queue. Each case shows the line “Customer needs help” and a link to the chat. Staff spend several minutes reading each chat before they can act. Some of them ask the customer to explain the problem again. What should the agent <b>send with each escalation</b>?", "options": [{"t": "The full chat transcript, pasted into the case in place of the link.", "why": "Works, but staff still read the whole chat to find the facts. It saves a click, not the minutes.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "A paragraph written by the agent that describes the problem in its own words.", "why": "Works, but free text varies. Some paragraphs leave out the box or the amount, and staff still have to hunt or ask.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "A summary with set fields: customer ID, cause, refund amount, next step.", "why": "Best. Staff see the facts they act on in the same place every time, without reading the chat."}, {"t": "The customer's account details and recent boxes, pulled fresh from <code>get_customer</code>.", "why": "Works, but different problem. It gives staff data, not the diagnosis: what went wrong and what the agent recommends.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Staff spend several minutes reading each chat before they can act.", "explanation": "Escalations should carry a structured handoff summary (customer ID, root cause, refund amount, recommended action) so a human can act without reading the transcript.", "eli5": {"question": "A nurse hands a patient to the next shift by saying “they need help, read the file.”", "answer": "Good handovers use a short card with the same boxes every time: who, what's wrong, what's been done, what to do next. The next nurse acts in seconds. <b>Rule: hand over to a human with a structured summary, not the transcript.</b>", "options": ["Handing over the whole file still means reading the whole file.", "A free-written note helps, but each one leaves out something different.", "Correct. The same short card, every time.", "The patient's records are useful, but they don't say what's wrong today."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>more information makes a better handoff</b>. Staff don't need more; they need the few facts they act on, in a fixed place. A structured summary lets them act without reading the transcript.", "diagram": "escalate_to_human(\n  customer_id, cause, refund_amount,\n  recommended_action )\n→ staff act without opening the chat", "mapTitle": "What does a good handoff carry?", "map": {"head": ["Handoff", "What staff do", "Result"], "rows": [["Link only", "Read the whole chat", "Minutes per case"], ["Full transcript", "Read the whole chat", "Minutes per case"], ["Free-text paragraph", "Read, sometimes ask again", "Facts vary"], ["Account data only", "Work out the problem", "No diagnosis"], ["Structured summary with set fields", "Act on it", "Fast and consistent"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each case shows the line “Customer needs help” and a link to the chat", "means": "The handoff carries no facts, only a pointer to them."}, {"quote": "Staff spend several minutes reading each chat before they can act", "means": "The decider: the cost is reading. Pasting the transcript in (<b>[[0]]</b>) keeps that cost."}, {"quote": "Some of them ask the customer to explain the problem again", "means": "Facts are missing. Free text (<b>[[1]]</b>) sometimes misses them too; account data (<b>[[3]]</b>) has no diagnosis."}, {"quote": "What should the agent send with each escalation?", "means": "Fixed fields staff act on: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Handing a case to a human? Send a structured summary they can act on without the transcript.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Implement multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "compile structured handoff summaries (customer ID, root cause, refund amount, recommended action) for humans without the transcript."}}}, {"id": "tg-who-escalates-3", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.2 Several customer matches", "trap": "Unreliable proxy", "fam": 6, "select": 1, "question": "A customer writes in from a new email address and gives only their name. <code>get_customer</code> returns two active accounts with that name. The agent picks the account with the most recent order and carries on. Last week this sent a refund to the wrong customer. How should the agent handle a lookup that returns <b>more than one customer</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Escalate each lookup with several matches to a person, who checks which account it is.", "why": "Works, but costs resolution. A person checks what the customer could confirm in one reply, and the queue grows.", "trap": "escalates what it could resolve"}, {"t": "Ask the customer for an identifier, such as a box number or postcode, first.", "why": "Best. The customer knows which account is theirs. One question settles it, and the agent can then resolve the case itself."}, {"t": "Pick the match whose postcode and recent boxes fit the chat best, and note the choice.", "why": "Works, but still a guess. Better clues pick right more often, and a wrong pick still refunds a stranger.", "trap": "unreliable proxy"}, {"t": "Have Claude rate how sure it is of the match, and ask the customer when it is unsure.", "why": "Works, but an unreliable signal. Claude was already sure enough to refund the wrong account.", "trap": "unreliable proxy"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "get_customer returns two active accounts with that name.", "explanation": "When a lookup returns several customers, ask the customer for an identifier rather than choosing by heuristic. A guess, however good, sometimes acts on the wrong account.", "eli5": {"question": "Two people called Sam Lee have parcels at the post office. The clerk hands over the newer parcel to whoever says “Sam Lee”.", "answer": "The clerk should ask “what's your postcode?” One question, and the right Sam gets the right parcel. <b>Rule: several matches → ask for an identifier, don't pick.</b>", "options": ["Calling a supervisor works, but the customer could answer it in one line.", "Correct. Ask for something only the right Sam knows.", "A smarter guess is still a guess.", "The clerk felt sure last time too."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a smarter rule can pick the right account</b>. Any rule is a proxy, and the cost of a wrong pick is a refund to a stranger. The customer has the missing fact, so ask them.", "diagram": "get_customer(\"Priya Shah\") → 2 accounts\n  ask: box number or postcode? → 1 account → resolve", "mapTitle": "Unsure who or what? Ask, guess, or escalate?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Agent's move", "Why"], "rows": [["Several customer matches", "Ask for an identifier", "The customer knows; don't guess"], ["Request is unclear", "Ask a clarifying question", "Cheap and exact"], ["Policy exception or gap", "Escalate", "Needs a human decision"], ["Customer asks for a person", "Escalate at once", "Explicit trigger"], ["Agent feels unsure", "Not a trigger by itself", "Self-rated confidence is unreliable"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "get_customer returns two active accounts with that name", "means": "The decider: the lookup is ambiguous, and both accounts are live."}, {"quote": "The agent picks the account with the most recent order", "means": "A heuristic. Better clues (<b>[[2]]</b>) or a confidence rating (<b>[[3]]</b>) are still guesses."}, {"quote": "Last week this sent a refund to the wrong customer", "means": "A wrong guess costs money, so guessing better is not enough."}, {"quote": "How should the agent handle a lookup that returns more than one customer?", "means": "Ask the customer, then resolve it: <b>[[1]]</b>. Escalating (<b>[[0]]</b>) hands over a case one question would settle."}], "rule": "Several matches? Ask the customer for an identifier; never pick by heuristic.", "guide": {"obj": "5.2 Escalation and ambiguity resolution patterns", "quote": "Multiple customer matches → ask for identifiers (not heuristic selection)."}}}]};
window.BEFORE = {"m7-s1-03": {"question": "Cancellations are handled by people, so the agent routes them through <code>escalate_to_human</code>. Product wants the agent to offer a pause before it escalates a cancellation. This is a retention nudge, not a policy. Missing it in some chats is acceptable. The change should be light to maintain. Which <b>TWO</b> changes fit best?", "options": [{"t": "Force <code>pause_subscription</code> through <code>tool_choice</code> on the turn where a customer first asks to cancel.", "why": "Incorrect. Forcing the tool pauses the subscription without the customer agreeing. The goal is to offer a pause, not to apply one."}, {"t": "Add a system-prompt instruction to suggest pausing deliveries before routing a cancellation request to a person.", "why": "Correct. A soft goal where occasional misses are fine is exactly what prompt guidance is for. It is one line to maintain."}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that denies <code>escalate_to_human</code> on cancellations until a pause has been offered.", "why": "Runner-up. A hook is the right tool when a step must happen every time. Here misses are acceptable, so code that must detect cancellations and offers is more machinery than the goal needs."}, {"t": "Include two or three sample chats where the agent suggests a pause and then escalates once the customer declines.", "why": "Correct. A few examples show the order of steps and the tone, and make the nudge consistent without any code."}], "answer": [1, 3], "runnerUp": 2}, "m7-s1-06": {"question": "<code>lookup_order</code> takes a box ID, and its whole description reads “Looks up a box”. Customers usually name a box by when it came, such as “last Tuesday's box”. The agent then passes that date as the box ID, and the call fails. Each customer's box IDs are listed in the <code>get_customer</code> record. What is the <b>most effective first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Expand the description with the ID format, a sample call, and how to go from a date to an ID.", "why": "Correct. Descriptions are the main way the model learns how to call a tool. This one gives no input format and no route from a date to an ID, and fixing that is cheap."}, {"t": "Return a structured validation error from <code>lookup_order</code> that names the expected ID format and an example.", "why": "Incorrect. A clear validation error helps the agent recover after a bad call. The first step is to stop the bad call."}, {"t": "Add a few-shot example to the system prompt that resolves a date-based request through <code>get_customer</code> first.", "why": "Runner-up. Examples help once the descriptions are clear but behaviour is still inconsistent. With a one-line description, the root cause is the description, and examples add tokens without fixing it."}, {"t": "Add a <code>lookup_order_by_date</code> tool that accepts a delivery date and returns the customer's matching box.", "why": "Incorrect. A new tool is more work than a first step, and with minimal descriptions the agent may misuse it too."}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 2}, "m7-s2-02": {"question": "Accessibility conventions apply to every screen component. Screens are files named <code>*.screen.tsx</code>. Most sit in feature folders under <code>mobile/</code>, but some live in <code>packages/shared/ui/</code>. Today the conventions are in the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, so they load even for server work. Where should the conventions <b>go</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Into <code>mobile/CLAUDE.md</code>, plus a <code>packages/shared/ui/CLAUDE.md</code> file that imports it.", "why": "Runner-up. Directory CLAUDE.md files work when a convention belongs to a folder. Screens are a filename pattern across trees: this needs a file per tree and also loads for non-screen files there."}, {"t": "Into an <code>/a11y</code> skill whose description says to use it whenever a screen file is edited.", "why": "Incorrect. Skills load on demand; relying on Claude to invoke one from its description is less dependable than a rule that loads by path."}, {"t": "Into a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file whose <code>paths:</code> frontmatter is a glob for that filename pattern.", "why": "Correct. A rule with a paths glob loads whenever Claude works on a matching file, wherever it sits, and stays out of server sessions."}, {"t": "Into a separate markdown file pulled into the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> through an <code>@import</code> line.", "why": "Incorrect. @import organises memory into modules, but an imported file still loads in every session."}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0}, "m7-s6-01": {"question": "Many certificates print no deductible at all. The schema marks deductible as a required number. For these certificates the model returns 0 or copies the limit. Either value then posts as if it had been printed. Account assistants cannot tell these values from real ones. Which change <b>best stops these invented values</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep the field required, and have the model enter -1 as a marker whenever no deductible is printed.", "why": "Incorrect. A sentinel number is still a number: downstream code must know the convention, and -1 can be posted as a real value."}, {"t": "Make deductible nullable in the schema, and describe in the field when to return null.", "why": "Correct. A required number forces the model to invent one. A nullable field gives it an honest way to report \"not printed\"."}, {"t": "Add two worked certificates with no printed deductible to the prompt, each with its expected output.", "why": "Runner-up. Examples reduce extraction hallucination and would be the fix if the field were already optional. While the schema demands a number, there is no valid output to show."}, {"t": "Add a deductible_status enum, printed or not_printed, beside the number, and route not_printed.", "why": "Incorrect. It labels the gap, but the required number still forces an invented value into the record."}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2}, "m7-s1-10": {"question": "Spoilage disputes can run long. When a chat nears the context limit, the SDK summarises older turns. Afterwards the agent sometimes offers a smaller credit than it promised earlier. It also forgets which items the customer listed. The tool results in these chats are short. Which change <b>best fixes this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep a running block of promised amounts, listed items and IDs, and put it at the top of every request.", "why": "Correct. Transactional facts kept outside the summarised history survive every summarisation pass, and placing them first keeps them prominent."}, {"t": "Trim each tool result to the few fields the agent uses before it is added to the conversation history.", "why": "Runner-up. Trimming is the fix when verbose tool output fills the context. These tool results are already short, so trimming saves little and the summaries still blur the promises."}, {"t": "Raise the context threshold at which older turns are summarised, keeping more of each chat verbatim.", "why": "Incorrect. This delays the loss rather than preventing it; long disputes still cross the threshold."}, {"t": "Ask the summariser to write longer summaries that quote every amount and item from the earlier turns.", "why": "Incorrect. A better summary prompt helps, but facts still pass through a lossy step each time. A separate facts block avoids that step."}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1}, "m7-s1-04": {"question": "<code>get_customer</code> returns the full card number on file, because the billing team's tools need it. That team owns the server and will not change it. Twice, the support agent read a card number back to a customer. Security says a card number must not reach a customer in any chat. Which change <b>meets this requirement</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a system-prompt rule to refer to cards by their last four digits only, with two example replies to copy.", "why": "Runner-up. A clear rule with examples is the right fix for a should-usually behaviour. Security needs a must-never, and prompt compliance has a non-zero failure rate."}, {"t": "Register a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that masks the card field in each <code>get_customer</code> result before Claude reads it.", "why": "Correct. The hook rewrites the result before the model sees it, so the agent cannot repeat a number it never had. It needs no change to the billing team's server."}, {"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that logs any card number found in a tool result for security to review.", "why": "Incorrect. Right hook, wrong action: logging records exposure after the model has the number; it does not stop the number reaching a reply."}, {"t": "Route <code>get_customer</code> through a subagent whose definition returns only name, plan and box IDs.", "why": "Incorrect. The subagent still receives the full number and decides what to pass on; its definition is an instruction, not a filter."}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0}};
